QUESTIONS.push(
/* ---------------- Pulmonary toxicity ---------------- */
{id:"L15-001", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"pulm", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"lungs-why-susceptible", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following are reasons the lungs are susceptible to toxic exposures? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Oxidative burden", correct:true, why:"Inhaled oxygen and oxygen-containing pollutant gases, plus the reactive oxygen species released by white blood cells, make the lung a very reactive place."},
  {t:"The chemistry of an inhaled gas sets where in the lung it goes", correct:true, why:"Gas exposures: the water solubility of a gas determines its site of deposition."},
  {t:"Particle size, shape and breathing pattern set where particles deposit", correct:true, why:"Particle exposures: size determines deposition, breathing patterns alter it, airway diameter matters and clearance matters."},
  {t:"The lungs have their own resident Kupffer cells", correct:false, why:"Kupffer cells are the liver's resident white blood cells."},
  {t:"The lungs carry out most of the body's drug metabolism", correct:false, why:"The liver is the workhorse of metabolism; the lung's problem is what it breathes in and the oxidative burden."}],
 teach:"The lungs are exposed to everything breathed in, all the time. Three things make them susceptible: the oxidative burden (oxygen, oxygen-containing pollutant gases, and the reactive oxygen species released by white blood cells responding to irritants or pathogens), gas exposures (the chemistry of the gas sets how far down it travels), and particle exposures (size, shape and breathing pattern set where particles deposit, and clearance decides what stays).",
 quote:"then these ideas of oxidative burden, the fact that we're exposed to different kinds of gasses, their chemistry is important, and particles size, shape, and, um, uh, yeah, size, shape, and our breathing patterns really affect our particle, our particle deposition and where our lungs are exposed, OK.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Pulmonary Toxicity – Why are lungs susceptible?’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-002", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"pulm", skill:"apply",
 concept:"oxidative-burden-sources", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"The oxidative burden of the lungs comes from two sources. Which pair is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"Inhaled reactive gases and the white blood cells that respond to them", correct:true, why:"Oxygen and pollutant gases such as sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides become reactive as they are metabolized; white blood cells fighting pathogens or cleaning up damage release reactive oxygen species too."},
  {t:"Hepatic first-pass metabolites carried into the lung by the pulmonary circulation", correct:false, why:"First pass is a liver story; the lung's burden comes from what it breathes and from its own immune response."},
  {t:"Low oxygen tension in the alveoli", correct:false, why:"The problem is reactive oxygen, not a shortage of it."},
  {t:"Hemoglobin releasing oxygen into the alveolar lining", correct:false, why:"Hemoglobin picks oxygen up in the lung; it is not the source of the burden."},
  {t:"Surfactant breaking down with every breath", correct:false, why:"Surfactant plays no part in the oxidative burden; the reactive oxygen comes from inhaled oxygen and pollutant gases and from white blood cells responding to irritants or pathogens."}],
 teach:"Oxidative burden means the lung is full of reactive oxygen. One source is what is inhaled: oxygen itself and pollutant gases with oxygen in the molecule (sulfur dioxide, nitrogen oxides), which become reactive as they are metabolized. The other is the lung's own defense: white blood cells drawn in by irritants or pathogens release reactive oxygen species as they work. Free electrons then damage the lipids and proteins of lung cells.",
 quote:"breathing in oxygen and other what we call reactive oxygen species exposes, thanks, exposes our lungs to just nasty reactive compounds. ... the activity of those white blood cells will also release reactive oxygen species. OK, so you have, therefore what we call a big oxidative burden.",
 note:"The transcript renders sulfur dioxide as \"s solar dioxide\" and says \"nitrous oxides\".",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Pulmonary Toxicity – Why are lungs susceptible?’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-003", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"pulm", skill:"apply",
 concept:"gas-chemistry-deposition", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient's lungs are injured by an unknown inhaled gas. Which property of the gas decides how far down the airway it travels, so that the location of the injury hints at the exposure?",
 options:[
  {t:"Its water solubility", correct:true, why:"The chemistry of the gas sets its site of deposition; in her words, very hydrophilic gases go quite far down into the lungs and hydrophobic gases do not."},
  {t:"Its molecular weight", correct:false, why:"Molecular weight is not what sets a gas's path; its chemistry, its water solubility, does. Size is what governs where particles deposit, not gases."},
  {t:"Its color", correct:false, why:"Color plays no part; how far a gas travels down the airway depends on how hydrophilic or hydrophobic it is."},
  {t:"Its temperature on inhalation", correct:false, why:"Temperature does not decide the depth a gas reaches; its water solubility does, with very hydrophilic gases going quite far down and hydrophobic ones not."},
  {t:"The breathing rate alone", correct:false, why:"Breathing pattern changes particle deposition; for gases the chemistry decides."}],
 teach:"A gas's chemistry, its water solubility, sets how far down the airway it travels: very hydrophilic gases go quite far down into the lungs, and hydrophobic gases do not. Because the depth reached is fixed by the chemistry, the site of damage in an injured lung points back to the kind of gas inhaled, which helps when no one knows the exposure.",
 quote:"the chemistry of the gas will determine where in the lungs that gas goes. OK? So, very hydrophilic gasses. Will actually go quite far down into the lungs and your hydrophobic gasses do not, OK, so the chemistry of the gas depends how far down it goes.",
 note:"Keyed on her words: hydrophilic gases travel farther down. The slide says only that water solubility of gases determines the site of deposition; see notes/L15.md for the direction question.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Pulmonary Toxicity – Why are lungs susceptible?’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-004", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"pulm", skill:"apply",
 concept:"particle-size-medium", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Inhaled particles are grouped as small, medium and large. Which group is the most concerning, and why?",
 options:[
  {t:"Medium: heavy enough not to be breathed back out, small enough to turn the corners into the lower lung", correct:true, why:"Medium particles reach the lower lung and settle out of the air as the breath stills, leaving them behind."},
  {t:"Large: carried deepest by the speed of inhalation", correct:false, why:"Large particles come in fast and impact at the bifurcations of the upper airways; they cannot turn the corners."},
  {t:"Small: they settle in the alveoli the moment the breath stills", correct:false, why:"Small particles are so light that they do not get past the nose and upper throat and are breathed back out."},
  {t:"Large: they impact at the bifurcations of the upper airways and are then carried on down into the alveoli", correct:false, why:"They stick where they impact; they do not go on to the alveoli."},
  {t:"Small: they are the only size that carries metals", correct:false, why:"Any particle can be a chemical particle carrying metals or organic compounds; size sets where it lands, not what it carries."}],
 teach:"Very small particles are breathed in and breathed back out without getting past the upper airway. Large particles come in fast and impact at the bifurcations of the upper airways. Medium particles are the concern: heavy enough not to be exhaled, small enough to go around the curves of the bronchioles, they reach the lower lung and fall out of the air during the still moment between inhaling and exhaling. Particles are also chemical exposures (dusts containing metals or organic compounds), so where they deposit is where the damage is.",
 quote:"The medium sized particles are the ones that are the most concerning. Um, they are heavy enough to get, to not be breathed out with every, every breath, um, and they are small enough that they can go around the curves in your bronchioles in your lung, and they get down into the lower parts of our lung",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Pulmonary Toxicity – Why are lungs susceptible?’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-005", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"pulm", skill:"apply",
 concept:"particle-shape-asbestos", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Asbestos causes mesothelioma on the outer lining of the lung. Which particle property explains how the particles get there?",
 options:[
  {t:"Their long, thin, sharp shape", correct:true, why:"Long thin particles embed in the upper airways where the lung bifurcates and can move through the lung tissue to its outer lining."},
  {t:"Their water solubility", correct:false, why:"Solubility is the gas story; asbestos is a particle."},
  {t:"Their very small size, which lets them be exhaled", correct:false, why:"Exhaled particles do no harm; asbestos fibers stay and migrate."},
  {t:"Their roundness", correct:false, why:"Round or cuboid particles do not embed the way long thin ones do."},
  {t:"Their metal content", correct:false, why:"Shape, not metal content, is what she used to explain mesothelioma."}],
 teach:"Shape matters as well as size. Long, thin particles embed in the upper airways at the bifurcations, and some, like asbestos fibers, which are sharp, move through the lung tissue and cause mesothelioma on the outer lining of the lung.",
 quote:"Those long thin particles will be also the ones that will be embedded into those upper airways as the lungs bifurcate, and some of those particles can move through tissues. I'm sure you guys have heard of mesothelioma with asbestos exposure.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Pulmonary Toxicity – Why are lungs susceptible?’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-006", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"pulm", skill:"apply",
 concept:"breathing-pattern-deposition", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why is jogging in a park a better choice than jogging beside a busy road?",
 options:[
  {t:"Heavier breathing while running deposits more exhaust particles in the lungs", correct:true, why:"Breathing patterns alter particle deposition: a runner breathes far more heavily than a walker, so the same polluted air delivers more to the lung."},
  {t:"Exhaust gases are hydrophobic, so they stay in the upper airway and never reach the lower lung", correct:false, why:"The point is the breathing pattern, not the chemistry of exhaust."},
  {t:"Running slows particle clearance by the cilia", correct:false, why:"Ciliary clearance decides what stays in the lung, but it is not what changes between walking and jogging; the jogger breathes more heavily and so takes in more exhaust."},
  {t:"Parks have a lower oxygen content", correct:false, why:"The difference between the park and the road is pollution, not oxygen: beside a busy road a heavily breathing jogger inhales far more car exhaust."},
  {t:"Road dust is too large to be inhaled", correct:false, why:"Large particles are inhaled and impact in the upper airways."}],
 teach:"Breathing patterns change how much of an exposure reaches the lung. A walker breathes lightly; a jogger breathes heavily, so running beside traffic means inhaling a great deal of exhaust. Her advice is to run where the air is cleaner.",
 quote:"If you are, um, walking, you're not going to breathe as heavily as you will if you're jogging. So I always like to suggest to people, um, jog in a place where there's not a lot of air pollution, so go jog in a park rather than beside a road.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Pulmonary Toxicity – Why are lungs susceptible?’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-007", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"pulm", skill:"tell", multi:true,
 concept:"acute-vs-chronic-pulmonary", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following are chronic responses to pulmonary exposures? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Fibrosis", correct:true, why:"An increase in collagen that reduces the plasticity of the lung: chronic."},
  {t:"Emphysema", correct:true, why:"Lungs become larger and too plastic, with hyperinflation and air trapping: chronic."},
  {t:"Asthma", correct:true, why:"Asthma is counted as a chronic response to pulmonary exposures."},
  {t:"Airway reactivity", correct:false, why:"Bronchoconstriction with wheezing, coughing, chest tightness and dyspnea is an acute response."},
  {t:"Pulmonary edema", correct:false, why:"The acute exudative phase that thickens the alveolar-capillary barrier is an acute response."}],
 teach:"Pulmonary toxicity is sorted into acute and chronic responses. Acute: airway reactivity (bronchoconstriction, decreased airway diameter, wheezing, coughing, chest tightness, dyspnea) and pulmonary edema (an acute exudative phase that thickens the alveolar-capillary barrier; abnormalities may persist). Chronic: fibrosis (more collagen, less plasticity), emphysema (lungs larger and too plastic, hyperinflation, reduced gas exchange from tissue loss and air trapping) and asthma.",
 quote:"airway reactivity, pulmonary edema are acute, and then fibrosis, emphysema and asthma are considered chronic results of exposures.",
 note:"A student asked her to repeat the classification; this is her second statement of it.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Types of Pulmonary Toxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-008", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"pulm", skill:"apply",
 concept:"systemic-pulmonary-toxins", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Bleomycin and cyclophosphamide are given intravenously and cause pulmonary fibrosis. What does this show about pulmonary toxicity?",
 options:[
  {t:"It can follow a systemic exposure, not only an inhaled one", correct:true, why:"Most pulmonary toxins are airborne, but some systemic compounds, including these two chemotherapy drugs, damage the lungs."},
  {t:"Intravenous drugs reach the lung first and so act as airborne toxins", correct:false, why:"They are systemic exposures; the point is that the lung can be the target of something that never entered by inhalation."},
  {t:"Chemotherapy drugs are the only systemic pulmonary toxins", correct:false, why:"Monocrotaline and paraquat, herbicides, cause pulmonary fibrosis as well."},
  {t:"Fibrosis is an acute response", correct:false, why:"Fibrosis is a chronic, long-suffering condition."},
  {t:"Particle size determines where these drugs deposit", correct:false, why:"They arrive in the blood; particle deposition does not apply."}],
 teach:"It is correct to think of pulmonary toxicity mostly as a response to inhaled gases and particles (airborne pulmonary toxins: particulates, oxygen). But pharmacists must know the systemic pulmonary toxins too: bleomycin and cyclophosphamide, chemotherapy drugs given by IV, cause pulmonary fibrosis, and the herbicides monocrotaline and paraquat, which some people have used to harm themselves, do the same.",
 quote:"bleomycin. And cyclophosphamide, these are chemotherapy medications. We give them by IV, but they're known to cause pulmonary toxicity. ... Remember, they're given by IV. They're a systemic exposure. They cause pulmonary fibrosis, which is a form of pulmonary toxicity, OK?",
 note:"The transcript renders monocrotaline as \"monochrotylin\".",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Types of Pulmonary Toxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-009", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"pulm", skill:"tell",
 concept:"fibrosis-non-organ-specific", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Bleomycin damages the lungs, yet the damage it causes is classed as a non-organ-specific toxic response. Why?",
 options:[
  {t:"The damage is fibrosis, which is classed by its mechanism rather than by the organ", correct:true, why:"Fibrosis, excess collagen and matrix deposition, is a non-organ-specific endpoint wherever it occurs; in the lung it is also a form of pulmonary toxicity."},
  {t:"Bleomycin is inhaled as an aerosol during chemotherapy, so it is an airborne pulmonary toxin", correct:false, why:"Bleomycin is given by IV."},
  {t:"The lungs are not an organ with site-specific toxicity", correct:false, why:"The lungs are one of the four organs with site-specific toxic responses."},
  {t:"It causes pulmonary edema, which affects every organ", correct:false, why:"The response is fibrosis, not edema."},
  {t:"Only the kidney can show an organ-specific response", correct:false, why:"Lungs, nervous system, liver and kidney all have site-specific responses."}],
 teach:"Toxic endpoints are sorted by how the damage happens, not only by where. Fibrosis is the same process wherever it occurs: excessive deposition of collagen and extracellular matrix protein, with loss of organ function as tissue is destroyed. That one pathology appears in the lung (bleomycin, amiodarone, paraquat) and in the liver as cirrhosis (alcohol), so fibrosis counts as non-organ-specific even though bleomycin's fibrosis is also a form of pulmonary toxicity.",
 quote:"because of the mechanism of how that damage occurs, it's considered a non-organ specific. So I'm making it a little confusing for you right here. Fibrosis, yeah, both of these compounds cause fibrosis.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Types of Pulmonary Toxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-010", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"pulm", skill:"apply",
 concept:"halogen-gas-acute", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A worker inhales fluorine or chlorine gas. The pulmonary response is best described as:",
 options:[
  {t:"Acute and caustic, driven by the water in the lungs", correct:true, why:"Her answer to a student's question: a halogen gas is very irritating, definitely acute, and caustic in the wet lung."},
  {t:"Chronic fibrosis appearing years later", correct:false, why:"Fibrosis is a chronic response; a halogen gas exposure is an acute one."},
  {t:"Emphysema from air trapping", correct:false, why:"Emphysema is chronic."},
  {t:"A delayed effect without immediate symptoms", correct:false, why:"The response is immediate and irritating."},
  {t:"A systemic response after absorption into the blood", correct:false, why:"The damage is at the site of contact in the airway."}],
 teach:"Fluorine and chlorine, halogens, are very irritating gases, and the water in the lungs makes them very caustic there. The injury happens at the site of first contact as the gas is breathed in (chlorine gas is the slide example of a local effect), so the result is an acute response rather than a chronic condition such as fibrosis or emphysema. Chlorine gas releases from accidents such as train derailments are real examples.",
 quote:"It would be definitely an acute response. It'll be very caustic to the lungs because of the water in our lungs. Yeah, fluorine gas, not a good thing to be exposed to. Yeah, like chlorine gas, any of those, um, halogen in the periodic table",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Types of Pulmonary Toxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

/* ---------------- Neurotoxicity ---------------- */
{id:"L15-011", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"neuro", skill:"figure",
 concept:"bbb-vs-systemic-capillary", tags:["smith"], source:"both", simg:"smith-bbb", simgAlt:"Casarett and Doull Figure 16-1: a systemic capillary and a brain capillary in cross-section.",
 stem:"In this figure, what makes the brain capillary a barrier?",
 options:[
  {t:"Tight junctions in place of fenestrations, and tightly connected glial cells around it", correct:true, why:"Solutes cannot slip between the vessel cells, and the glial cells outside the vessel are tightly joined, unlike the loosely joined pericytes of a systemic capillary."},
  {t:"Larger fenestrations that let only water through", correct:false, why:"Brain capillaries have tight junctions instead of the fenestrations seen in systemic capillaries."},
  {t:"A thicker layer of loosely joined pericytes outside the vessel, with larger fenestrations in its wall", correct:false, why:"Loosely joined pericytes are the systemic capillary; the brain capillary is wrapped by tightly connected glial cells."},
  {t:"A myelin sheath around the vessel", correct:false, why:"Myelin wraps axons, not capillaries."},
  {t:"The absence of any surrounding cells", correct:false, why:"The surrounding glial cells are part of the barrier."}],
 teach:"A systemic capillary has openings called fenestrations and is surrounded by pericytes that are not tightly joined, so solutes move out of the blood easily. A brain capillary has tight junctions where the vessel cells touch, and it is surrounded by glial cells that are tightly connected to each other. That tightness is the blood–brain barrier, which keeps out larger and non-lipophilic compounds.",
 quote:"in the brain, you have capillaries again. And you have, instead of large fenestration sites, you've got very tight junctions where the cells of the vessel are tightly touching ... You also have these brain capillaries surrounded by glial cells. They are much more tightly connected to each other",
 note:"The transcript renders pericytes as \"parasites\".",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Neurotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-012", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"neuro", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"bbb-properties", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statements about the blood–brain barrier are correct? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Penetration depends on a compound's lipophilicity", correct:true, why:"Larger and non-lipophilic compounds do not have the chemistry to get through the vessel wall and the glial cells."},
  {t:"It is not continuous: some parts of the brain are looser than others", correct:true, why:"The barrier is not always as tight as it is thought to be."},
  {t:"It is still developing at birth", correct:true, why:"For several months to years, so drugs that stay out of an adult brain can enter a child's."},
  {t:"It is fully formed at birth", correct:false, why:"It is still developing at birth."},
  {t:"It keeps out lipophilic compounds preferentially", correct:false, why:"Lipophilic compounds are the ones that cross; the barrier protects against non-lipophilic and larger ones."}],
 teach:"Because tight junctions and tightly joined glial cells close the gaps between cells, a compound has to pass through the endothelial cell membranes themselves, so penetration depends on its lipophilicity: larger and non-lipophilic compounds are kept out, and transporters move some compounds back to the blood. The barrier is not continuous; some parts of the brain are looser than others. And it is still developing at birth for several months to years, so a drug kept out of an adult brain can cross into a child's.",
 quote:"it's protecting us from exposures to. Especially non-lipophilic compounds or larger compounds ... There are parts of the brain where the blood-brain barrier is like, I guess you'd say looser than others. ... That blood-brain barrier is still developing at birth for several months to years.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Neurotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-013", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"neuro", skill:"apply",
 concept:"bbb-newborn", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A medication that causes no nervous-system harm in adults harmed newborns who received it. Which feature of the blood–brain barrier explains this?",
 options:[
  {t:"It is still developing at birth", correct:true, why:"For months to years after birth the barrier is immature, so a drug that is kept out of the adult brain can cross into the child's."},
  {t:"It is tighter in children than in adults", correct:false, why:"The reverse: it is less complete in the newborn."},
  {t:"Children have more fenestrations in systemic capillaries", correct:false, why:"Systemic capillaries are not the barrier in question."},
  {t:"Newborns lack transporters that pump drugs into the brain", correct:false, why:"Transporters move compounds back to the blood; the issue is the immature barrier."},
  {t:"Lipophilic drugs cannot cross in adults", correct:false, why:"Lipophilic drugs cross at any age; the newborn's barrier also lets other drugs through."}],
 teach:"Before this was understood, some drugs given to very young children crossed the developing blood–brain barrier and caused harm, while the same drugs were no problem in adults whose barrier was tight and complete. Pediatric dosing has to allow for an immature barrier.",
 quote:"We would give some drugs to a child, a very young child. It would cross the blood-brain barrier and cause harm, whereas that same medication was not an issue for adults where their blood-brain barrier was more, more, um, um, uh, uh, tight and contained.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Neurotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-014", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"neuro", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"ns-why-susceptible", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which features make the nervous system susceptible to neurotoxins? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Very high energy requirements and dependence on aerobic metabolism", correct:true, why:"Anything that reduces oxygen or energy available to neurons causes trouble."},
  {t:"Axonal transport along very long cells", correct:true, why:"Nutrients and waste must move up and down axons that run from the spinal cord down the limbs."},
  {t:"Myelin, found in no other organ, which can be damaged", correct:true, why:"The Schwann-cell sheath speeds conduction but is a target."},
  {t:"Dependence on neurotransmission to do its work", correct:true, why:"Release, binding and reuptake of neurotransmitters can each be disturbed."},
  {t:"Resident Kupffer cells", correct:false, why:"Kupffer cells belong to the liver."},
  {t:"A urine-concentrating mechanism", correct:false, why:"That is why the kidney is susceptible."}],
 teach:"Each feature is a point a toxin can hit. Neurons have very high energy needs and depend on aerobic metabolism, so any exposure that cuts oxygen or energy causes nervous-system problems. Their long axons must transport nutrients and waste up and down, which adds to that energy demand. Myelin, found in no other organ, speeds conduction, but if a toxin damages the Schwann cells, transmission slows (tingling hands and feet). And the nervous system alone works by neurotransmission, so disturbing release, crossing of the cleft or reuptake produces symptoms.",
 quote:"So high energy requirements, those long axons need to have the ability to transport nutrients and waste up and down them. You've got the presence of the myelin sheath. It's usually protective and very helpful for us, but sometimes those Schwann cells can get damaged, and then neurotransmission is the way our nervous system works. If it gets disturbed, our nervous system isn't going to function, OK.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slides ‘Susceptibility to Neurotoxicity – Energy and Axons’, ‘Susceptibility to Neurotoxicity - Myelin’, ‘Susceptibility to Neurotoxicity - Neurotransmission’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-015", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"neuro", skill:"apply",
 concept:"myelinopathy-metronidazole", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"During a second, higher-dose course of metronidazole (Flagyl) for Giardia, a patient's hands and feet begin to tingle and nothing else is affected. Which pattern of neurotoxicity is this?",
 options:[
  {t:"Myelinopathy", correct:true, why:"Her own story: the drug damages the Schwann cells that make myelin, conduction slows, and the symptom is tingling of the hands and feet."},
  {t:"Neuronopathy", correct:false, why:"Death of whole neurons is not what tingling from a reversible drug effect reflects."},
  {t:"Axonopathy", correct:false, why:"Axonopathy is degeneration of the axon itself with the cell body surviving; her symptom came from damage to the myelinating Schwann cells, which slows conduction and shows up as tingling of the hands and feet."},
  {t:"Interruption of neurotransmission", correct:false, why:"That is the organophosphate pattern, not the metronidazole one."},
  {t:"Multiple sclerosis", correct:false, why:"Her first fear, but the cause was the medication, and stopping it resolved it."}],
 teach:"Myelin damage slows neurotransmission, and the classic symptom is tingling of the hands and feet. Her example is personal: a second, higher-dose course of metronidazole for Giardia produced tingling in just her hands and feet; the drug causes myelinopathy, and she stopped it. Remyelination can occur but takes a long time, so myelinopathy may not be the worst kind of neurotoxicity.",
 quote:"it was actually the medication that I was being given, that medication will cause myelinopathy, and so, without talking to my doctor, I took myself off that drug. ... But yeah, myelinopathy, my hands and feet were tingling, it was strange.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slides ‘Susceptibility to Neurotoxicity - Myelin’, ‘Types of Neurotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-016", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"neuro", skill:"figure", type:"match",
 concept:"neurotoxicity-patterns", tags:["smith"], source:"both", simg:"smith-neuro-types", simgAlt:"Casarett and Doull figure of neurotoxic injury: a normal neuron (A) and four injured neurons (B to E).",
 stem:"Match each pattern of neurotoxic injury in the figure (neurons B through E) with what the toxin did to the cell.",
 left:["Neuronopathy", "Axonopathy", "Myelinopathy", "Neurotransmission toxicity"],
 right:["The entire neuron dies", "The axon degenerates; the cell body survives", "Myelin or the myelinating cells are damaged", "Excitation is blocked or excessive; the cell looks normal"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Neuronopathy", r:"The entire neuron dies", why:"Neuron B: the toxin killed the whole thing; astrocytes proliferate in response."},
  {l:"Axonopathy", r:"The axon degenerates; the cell body survives", why:"Neuron C: only the axon is damaged, as if chemically cut; the surviving neuron shows chromatolysis."},
  {l:"Myelinopathy", r:"Myelin or the myelinating cells are damaged", why:"Neuron D: the Schwann cells making the sheath are hit; conduction slows; remyelination is slow, and less effective in the CNS than the PNS."},
  {l:"Neurotransmission toxicity", r:"Excitation is blocked or excessive; the cell looks normal", why:"Neuron E: the process of neurotransmission is interrupted rather than the cell killed."}],
 teach:"Casarett and Doull's figure of patterns of neurotoxic injury shows a normal neuron (A) and four discrete kinds of damage: neuronopathy, death of the entire neuron (B); axonopathy, degeneration of the axon with the cell body surviving (C); myelinopathy, disruption of myelin or injury to the myelinating cells (D); and interruption of neurotransmission, by blocking excitation or by excessive stimulation, without cell death (E). A given neurotoxin usually causes one of these, and they look different under the microscope.",
 quote:"What did that neurotoxin do to this nerve cell? Killed the whole thing, right? ... What kind of damage are we calling that? Yeah, axonopathy. ... how about Cell D? What kind of neurotoxicity is this? Myelinopathy, yeah ... The neuron in E undergoing, what kind of neurotoxicity is happening there? Yeah, the neurotransmission is being disturbed.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Types of Neurotoxicity’ (Figure 16-2); transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-017", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"neuro", skill:"apply",
 concept:"organophosphate-neurotransmission", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Organophosphate pesticides are neurotoxins. Which pattern of neurotoxic injury do they produce?",
 options:[
  {t:"Interruption of neurotransmission", correct:true, why:"Her example for neuron E: a common class of pesticides that perturbs neurotransmission."},
  {t:"Neuronopathy", correct:false, why:"Neuronopathy is death of the entire neuron; organophosphates leave the cell intact and act at the synapse, where excess acetylcholine disturbs neurotransmission."},
  {t:"Axonopathy", correct:false, why:"Axonopathy is degeneration of the axon; organophosphates do not damage the axon but raise acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft, interrupting neurotransmission."},
  {t:"Myelinopathy", correct:false, why:"Myelin is not their target; metronidazole was her myelinopathy example."},
  {t:"All four patterns at once, in every exposed neuron", correct:false, why:"It is very rare for one compound to cause more than one discrete pattern."}],
 teach:"Organophosphates do not kill the neuron, cut the axon or strip the myelin; they disturb signaling at the synapse. The treatment slides give the mechanism: organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine builds up in the synaptic cleft and causes cholinergic excess. That is the neuron-E pattern, interruption of neurotransmission by excessive stimulation, in which the cell itself looks unchanged. Her other discrete-pattern example is metronidazole, which causes myelinopathy.",
 quote:"Can you think of any sort of compound you've learned about that perturbs neurotransmission? It's a neurotoxin. ... How about organophosphates, right? A common class of pesticides that we use, they will cause neurotransmission damage.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slides ‘Susceptibility to Neurotoxicity - Neurotransmission’, ‘Types of Neurotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-018", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"neuro", skill:"tell",
 concept:"neurotoxin-discrete-pattern", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which pattern of neurotoxicity leaves the neuron looking unchanged under the microscope, so that it is recognized by loss of function instead?",
 options:[
  {t:"Interruption of neurotransmission", correct:true, why:"The cell is intact; what is missing is the neurotransmission."},
  {t:"Neuronopathy", correct:false, why:"The dead neuron and the gliosis around it are visible."},
  {t:"Axonopathy", correct:false, why:"The degenerated axon and chromatolysis are visible."},
  {t:"Myelinopathy", correct:false, why:"The damaged sheath is visible."},
  {t:"None: every pattern changes the cell's appearance", correct:false, why:"The neurotransmission pattern does not."}],
 teach:"Most of the time a neurotoxin causes one discrete kind of injury; it is very rare for one compound to blast the myelin, disturb neurotransmission and kill the axon all at once. Neuronopathy, axonopathy and myelinopathy each look different under the microscope. Neurotransmission toxicity produces no change in the cell's appearance and is recognized by the lack of neurotransmission.",
 quote:"most of the time, a neurotoxin that you might be exposed to is gonna cause one of these discrete kinds of neurotoxicity. ... and then the neurotransmission. Really won't have any change to the look of the cell, but you'll definitely be able to figure that out because of the lack of, of neurotransmission going on.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Types of Neurotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

/* ---------------- Hepatotoxicity: why susceptible; kinds of injury ---------------- */
{id:"L15-019", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"hepat", skill:"recall",
 concept:"kupffer-cells-name", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The white blood cells that reside in the liver, waiting to clean up damage or fight pathogens, are called:",
 options:[
  {t:"Kupffer cells", correct:true, why:"She spelled it out: K U P F E R; a kind of white blood cell that lives in the liver."},
  {t:"Schwann cells", correct:false, why:"Schwann cells make myelin in the peripheral nervous system."},
  {t:"Pericytes", correct:false, why:"Pericytes surround systemic capillaries."},
  {t:"Hepatocytes", correct:false, why:"Hepatocytes are the metabolizing liver cells, not immune cells."},
  {t:"Astrocytes", correct:false, why:"Astrocytes are glial cells of the nervous system."}],
 teach:"One reason the liver is susceptible is that it has its own inflammatory cells, the Kupffer cells, a kind of resident white blood cell waiting to be called up to clean up dead tissue or fight pathogens.",
 quote:"Does anybody know what the name of the white blood cells are that hang out in the liver are called? ... The Cooperer cells, Cooper, K U P F E R, Coopfer cells. They're just kind, a kind of white blood cell that resides in the liver, waiting to be called up",
 note:"The transcript renders Kupffer as \"Cooper\"/\"Coopfer\".",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Hepatotoxicity – Why is liver susceptible?’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-020", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"hepat", skill:"apply",
 concept:"kupffer-cells-add-injury", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why does having its own resident immune cells make the liver more susceptible to injury from a hepatotoxin?",
 options:[
  {t:"Cleaning up damaged cells, they release inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species", correct:true, why:"The cleanup adds inflammation and reactive oxygen to the injury already under way."},
  {t:"They metabolize drugs to toxic metabolites", correct:false, why:"Metabolism is the hepatocytes' job; Kupffer cells are immune cells."},
  {t:"They block blood flow through the sinusoids", correct:false, why:"Kupffer cells do not add to injury by blocking flow; they add to it by releasing inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species while cleaning up damaged cells."},
  {t:"They take up and concentrate xenobiotics from the portal blood before the hepatocytes can metabolize them", correct:false, why:"Uptake and concentration is a separate reason for susceptibility, and it belongs to the liver cells generally."},
  {t:"They prevent regeneration of hepatocytes", correct:false, why:"Regeneration is not what they affect; the harm is the inflammation and reactive oxygen species released as they chew up dead and dying cells, which adds to the damage already under way."}],
 teach:"Kupffer cells are there to help, especially against pathogens. But when liver tissue is being damaged by an exposure, their job of chewing up dead or dying cells releases inflammatory cytokines (calling in more immune cells) and reactive oxygen species, which add to the inflammation and the mess.",
 quote:"The Cooper cells are there to help, but kind of as a, a side effect of their job, they can create a little bit more of a mess by the inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species they release doing their job of cleaning up pathogens or damaged cells, yeah.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Hepatotoxicity – Why is liver susceptible?’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-021", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"hepat", skill:"apply",
 concept:"idiosyncratic-liver-pharmacogenomics", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"One patient reacts severely and unexpectedly to a drug that everyone else tolerates. Which organ is most likely to show this kind of reaction, and what is increasingly explaining it?",
 options:[
  {t:"The liver; a rare genetic variant that changes the drug's metabolism", correct:true, why:"The liver is the organ most likely to have idiosyncratic reactions, and pharmacogenomics is turning some of them into explained reactions: a variant that makes a hepatotoxic metabolite."},
  {t:"The kidney; its sensitivity to vasoconstrictors", correct:false, why:"The kidneys do not tend to have idiosyncratic reactions."},
  {t:"The lungs; their oxidative burden", correct:false, why:"The lungs do not tend to have idiosyncratic reactions; oxidative burden makes every lung susceptible and does not explain one person's unexpected reaction. The liver, where a rare variant changes a drug's metabolism, is the prone organ."},
  {t:"The nervous system; its high energy requirement and dependence on aerobic metabolism", correct:false, why:"The CNS does not seem to have many idiosyncratic reactions."},
  {t:"The liver; its large size", correct:false, why:"Size has nothing to do with it; metabolism does."}],
 teach:"An idiosyncratic reaction is a strong, unexpected reaction in one person that most people do not have. The liver is the organ most prone to them because it does most of the body's drug metabolism: a person with a rare genetic variant may metabolize a drug differently from everyone else, perhaps to a hepatotoxic metabolite, and that happens in the liver rather than the kidneys or lungs. Pharmacogenomics is identifying such variants, so some once-unexplainable reactions are becoming explainable.",
 quote:"The liver is the organ in our body that tends to have that kind of reaction. The kidneys don't really do that. ... they've got a genetic variant that's making them metabolize that drug differently than the rest of us, and perhaps that metabolism resulted in a nasty hepatotoxic metabolite.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Hepatotoxicity – Why is liver susceptible?’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-022", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"hepat", skill:"figure",
 concept:"lobule-blood-flow", tags:["smith"], source:"both", simg:"smith-liver", simgAlt:"Liver anatomy: blood flow through the organs (A), the liver and its vessels (B), and a liver lobule (C).",
 stem:"In the liver lobule (panel C), which vessels carry blood in, and which carries it out?",
 options:[
  {t:"In by the hepatic artery and portal vein; out by the central vein", correct:true, why:"Blood trickles from the inflow vessels at the edge down the cords of hepatocytes and drains into the central vein."},
  {t:"In by the central vein at the middle of the lobule; out by the bile duct at the edge", correct:false, why:"The bile ducts begin at the edges of the lobe and carry bile, not blood; the central vein is the outflow."},
  {t:"In by the bile duct; out by the portal vein", correct:false, why:"Bile ducts carry bile; the portal vein is an inflow vessel."},
  {t:"In by the central vein; out by the hepatic artery", correct:false, why:"This reverses the direction of flow."},
  {t:"In and out by the portal vein alone", correct:false, why:"Two vessels bring blood in and the central vein takes it out."}],
 teach:"Blood comes into each lobe of the liver through the hepatic artery and the hepatic portal vein, which sit at the edges along with the start of the bile ducts. It flows down along the cords of hepatocytes and drains out through the central vein. Oral exposures reach the liver through the portal circulation, which is why first pass happens there. This direction of flow is the basis of the zones in the next slide.",
 quote:"you've got blood coming into each lobe through the hepatic portal artery and also the hepatic portal vein. You also have your bile ducts initiating on the edges of each of these lobes, and the blood will trickle down across those basically cords of hepatocytes ... and it drains out of the liver via the hepatic, the central vein, OK?",
 note:"She said \"hepatic portal artery\" for the hepatic artery.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Hepatotoxicity – review of liver anatomy’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-023", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"hepat", skill:"apply",
 concept:"discrete-hepatotoxicity-ethanol-aflatoxin", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the table of liver responses to xenobiotics, ethanol appears under hepatocyte death, steatosis, inflammation, regeneration and fibrosis, while aflatoxin appears only under liver cancers. What is the lesson?",
 options:[
  {t:"Most compounds cause one discrete kind of liver injury; ethanol is unusual in causing many", correct:true, why:"Each compound is usually discrete in the hepatotoxicity it causes; aflatoxin only causes liver cancer, while ethanol hits many parts of the liver."},
  {t:"Ethanol is the most potent hepatotoxin", correct:false, why:"Potency is not what the table shows; breadth of response is."},
  {t:"Aflatoxin is harmless at ordinary exposures", correct:false, why:"It is a very high risk for liver cancer."},
  {t:"The table must be memorized in full", correct:false, why:"Her joke; the table is not to be memorized."},
  {t:"Liver cancer is the usual endpoint for every hepatotoxin once the exposure goes on long enough", correct:false, why:"Each compound tends to cause its own discrete kind of liver damage, not cancer by default; liver cancer is aflatoxin's endpoint, while acetaminophen, for example, causes hepatocyte death."}],
 teach:"The table (Klaassen, Table 13-2) lists responses on the left and representative toxicants on the right. Ethanol can cause hepatocyte death, fatty liver (steatosis), inflammation, altered regeneration and fibrosis. Aflatoxin, a chemical made by molds growing on grains and the subject of her PhD work, causes liver cancer and little else. The point is that compounds, medications included, tend to cause very specific kinds of liver damage. The table is not to be memorized; the kinds of response are.",
 quote:"ethanol can cause fatty liver, also called steatosis. Ethanol can cause inflammation. Ethanol can cause affect liver regeneration, and ethanol can cause fibrosis, OK? Other compounds are not so. Uh, good at hitting many different parts of the liver, so things like aflatoxin ... So this aflatoxin really is only causing liver cancer.",
 note:"\"you have to memorize this table. It is all inclusive ... Do you think that's worth your time? I'm kidding. ... No, you do not have to memorize this table, OK?\"",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Site-Specific Hepatotoxicity’ (Klaassen Table 13-2); transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-024", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"hepat", skill:"recall", type:"match",
 concept:"liver-response-types", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Match each kind of liver response with its description.",
 left:["Hepatocyte death", "Steatosis", "Canalicular cholestasis", "Liver cancer"],
 right:["Death of the cells that do most of the metabolism (acetaminophen, ethanol)", "Fatty liver (ethanol)", "Bile flow blocked as the cells lining the canaliculi are damaged and inflamed", "The one response aflatoxin produces"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Hepatocyte death", r:"Death of the cells that do most of the metabolism (acetaminophen, ethanol)", why:"The actual death of the hepatocytes."},
  {l:"Steatosis", r:"Fatty liver (ethanol)", why:"Steatosis is the other name for fatty liver."},
  {l:"Canalicular cholestasis", r:"Bile flow blocked as the cells lining the canaliculi are damaged and inflamed", why:"The bile duct becomes cholestatic, blocked and narrowed."},
  {l:"Liver cancer", r:"The one response aflatoxin produces", why:"Aflatoxin is a strong risk for liver cancer and causes little else."}],
 teach:"The table sorts liver injury by what is damaged. Hepatocyte death is the actual death of the hepatocytes, the cells that do much of the metabolism (acetaminophen, ethanol). Fatty liver is also called steatosis. In canalicular cholestasis the cells lining the bile ducts are damaged and inflamed, so the duct narrows and bile is blocked. Because each compound tends to cause one discrete kind of damage, aflatoxin produces liver cancer and nothing else on the table.",
 quote:"hepatocyte death, that's the actual death of those, those hepatocytes, the ones that do a lot of the metabolism, OK? The canalicular cholestasis means that the bile duct gets blocked, OK? It becomes um cholestatic, it's blocked, it's, it, it gets narrowed because those cells lining the bile ducts get damaged and inflamed.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Site-Specific Hepatotoxicity’ (Klaassen Table 13-2); transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-025", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"hepat", skill:"apply",
 concept:"pattern-of-injury-biopsy", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient's liver function is falling and no one knows what the exposure was. How is the pattern of liver injury found, and what is it used for?",
 options:[
  {t:"Imaging and a liver biopsy; the pattern hints at the exposure", correct:true, why:"Different hepatotoxins cause different, discrete kinds of damage, so the pattern of injury points toward what the patient was exposed to."},
  {t:"Serum creatinine; it names the toxin directly", correct:false, why:"Creatinine is a kidney measure, and no test names the toxin by itself."},
  {t:"A chest X-ray; the lungs show the same pattern", correct:false, why:"Lung imaging does not show liver injury."},
  {t:"Liver function tests alone; a biopsy is never needed once the enzymes are elevated", correct:false, why:"Liver function tests show that the liver is affected; the pattern of injury takes imaging and biopsy."},
  {t:"Genotyping; every pattern has one gene", correct:false, why:"Genotyping can explain an idiosyncratic reaction, not identify the pattern of injury."}],
 teach:"Because each hepatotoxin causes a discrete kind of damage, the pattern of injury is a clue to the exposure. When liver function is declining and the exposure is unknown, clinicians use imaging and a liver biopsy to look for that pattern. She used to collect punch biopsies herself, done externally with a needle: a small core of tissue is enough to start working out what happened.",
 quote:"Often they'll do imaging and uh um uh a liver biopsy to look for the pattern of injury, and that pattern of injury will give us a hint of what they might have been exposed to, OK.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Site-Specific Hepatotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

/* ---------------- Hepatotoxicity: zones ---------------- */
{id:"L15-026", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"zones", skill:"recall",
 concept:"acinus-zones", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the acinar model of the liver, zone 3 is:",
 options:[
  {t:"Closest to the central vein, where blood leaves", correct:true, why:"Zone 1 is next to the inflow vessels, zone 2 is intermediate, zone 3 is next to the central venule drainage."},
  {t:"Closest to the hepatic artery and portal vein, where blood enters", correct:false, why:"That is zone 1."},
  {t:"The intermediate region between inflow and outflow", correct:false, why:"That is zone 2."},
  {t:"The bile duct region at the edge of the lobe", correct:false, why:"The bile ducts begin at the edges, next to zone 1."},
  {t:"The region with the highest oxygen content", correct:false, why:"Zone 1 has the highest oxygen; zone 3 the lowest."}],
 teach:"The acinus divides the liver by the path of the blood. Blood enters from the hepatic artery and portal vein, flows along the hepatocytes and drains into the central vein. Zone 1 is closest to the inflow vessels, zone 3 closest to the central vein, zone 2 between. Because zone 3 gets the blood last, after upstream hepatocytes have taken oxygen out, it is the low-oxygen zone; it is also where CYP2E1 is most highly expressed.",
 quote:"zone 1 is closest to the, the vessels where the blood comes into the liver, and zone 3 is closest to the vessel where the blood leaves the liver, OK?",
 note:"The transcript renders acinus as \"ainous\" and \"aceous\".",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Reasons for Site-Specific Hepatotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-027", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"zones", skill:"apply",
 concept:"oxygen-gradient", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why is the blood in zone 3 of the liver relatively hypoxic?",
 options:[
  {t:"The hepatocytes upstream take oxygen out of it as it flows from the inflow vessels to the central vein", correct:true, why:"Blood arrives relatively oxygenated, the hepatocytes grab oxygen as it passes, and it leaves through the central vein relatively hypoxic."},
  {t:"The central vein carries only venous blood from the gut, which has already lost its oxygen before it reaches the liver", correct:false, why:"The gradient forms inside the liver as hepatocytes use the oxygen."},
  {t:"Zone 3 has no blood supply", correct:false, why:"It is where the blood drains out."},
  {t:"Kupffer cells consume the oxygen in zone 3", correct:false, why:"Kupffer cells move all over the liver; the hepatocytes along the flow path use the oxygen."},
  {t:"Bile absorbs the oxygen", correct:false, why:"Bile plays no part; the oxygen is taken out of the blood by the hepatocytes it flows past on the way to the central vein."}],
 teach:"Blood entering from the hepatic artery and portal vein is relatively oxygenated. As it flows past the cords of hepatocytes, they take oxygen out of it, so by the time it reaches the central vein it is relatively hypoxic. There is therefore an oxygen gradient across the acinus, high in zone 1 and low in zone 3, and that gradient is one reason different hepatotoxins injure different zones.",
 quote:"As the blood flows past those hepatocytes, and the hepatocytes grab the oxygen out of the blood, as that blood drains out of the liver via the central vein, the blood is hypoxic, compared to what it was as it came into the liver.",
 note:"She illustrated with the figure's red and blue coloring (\"Look at your arms, right? Your veins. Your blood is blue.\"); a student objected, and she answered \"That's not the point ... The point is, is that oxygen comes into the liver, relatively oxygenated. It leaves the liver through the central vein, relatively hypoxic\".",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Reasons for Site-Specific Hepatotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-028", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"zones", skill:"apply",
 concept:"acetaminophen-zone3", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient has overdosed on acetaminophen and liver function tests show injury. On biopsy, which zone shows hepatocyte death, and why?",
 options:[
  {t:"Zone 3: CYP2E1, which makes NAPQI, is most highly expressed there", correct:true, why:"Her little case: the overflow of acetaminophen is bioactivated by CYP2E1, and CYP2E1 is highest in zone 3, so the hepatocytes there die."},
  {t:"Zone 1: it receives the drug first", correct:false, why:"Receiving the drug first is not what decides it; where the bioactivating enzyme lives does."},
  {t:"Zone 1: its high oxygen content drives NAPQI formation in the first hepatocytes the drug meets", correct:false, why:"Oxygen is high in zone 1, but CYP2E1 is in zone 3."},
  {t:"Zone 2: it is intermediate for every toxin", correct:false, why:"Zone 2 is only the region between inflow and outflow; acetaminophen injury follows CYP2E1, which is most highly expressed in zone 3."},
  {t:"All three zones equally", correct:false, why:"Metabolizing enzymes are not homogeneously expressed, so the damage is zonal."}],
 teach:"In an acetaminophen overdose, CYP2E1 bioactivates the drug to NAPQI, the hepatotoxic metabolite. The metabolite is made where the enzyme is, and CYP2E1 is expressed more highly in zone 3 than in zone 1, so zone 3 hepatocytes make the most NAPQI and are the ones that die. The zone of injury follows the location of the bioactivating enzyme, not the order in which the blood reaches the cells.",
 quote:"So if you were to do a biopsy of their liver. What area of the liver has hepatocyte death occurring? Zone 3 why Cuz that's where a lot of 20 is expressed. OK.",
 note:"The transcript renders CYP2E1 as \"20\", \"SIP2E1\" and \"cytokrome P452E1\", and NAPQI as \"NAQI\".",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Reasons for Site-Specific Hepatotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-029", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"zones", skill:"recall",
 concept:"zonal-enzyme-expression", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statement about metabolizing enzymes across the liver is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"They are not homogeneously expressed: many phase 2 enzymes sit in zone 1, and CYP2E1 is higher in zone 3", correct:true, why:"Enzyme expression is site-specific, which, with the oxygen gradient, explains why hepatotoxins damage particular zones."},
  {t:"All cytochrome P450s are expressed evenly across all three zones of the liver, so site-specific injury comes only from blood flow", correct:false, why:"That is the assumption she corrected; expression is zonal."},
  {t:"Phase 2 enzymes are confined to zone 3", correct:false, why:"Many phase 2 enzymes are in zone 1."},
  {t:"CYP2E1 is highest in zone 1", correct:false, why:"CYP2E1 is higher in zone 3."},
  {t:"Only Kupffer cells express metabolizing enzymes", correct:false, why:"Hepatocytes do the metabolism."}],
 teach:"Metabolizing enzymes are not spread evenly through the liver. Many phase 2 enzymes sit in zone 1, while some P450s, CYP2E1 among them, are more highly expressed in zone 3. A toxic metabolite is made where its enzyme is, so the hepatocytes in that zone take the damage: CYP2E1 in zone 3 is why acetaminophen kills zone 3 hepatocytes. With the oxygen gradient, this uneven expression explains why different hepatotoxins injure different regions.",
 quote:"a lot of our phase 2 enzymes are in zone 1. And some of our P-450s are more in zone 3. For example, cytokrome P452E1 is more highly expressed in zone 3. Then in zone one. ... So oxygen gradients and non-homogeneous expression of our metabolizing enzymes contributes to where you see liver damage for different hepatotoxins.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Reasons for Site-Specific Hepatotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

/* ---------------- Nephrotoxicity ---------------- */
{id:"L15-030", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"nephro", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"kidney-why-susceptible", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following make the kidneys susceptible to toxic injury? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"They receive a large delivery of blood, including metabolites made by the liver", correct:true, why:"Lots of blood flows to the kidney, and much of it has passed through the liver and carries new metabolites."},
  {t:"Concentrating urine also concentrates poisons in the tubule and in the tubular cells", correct:true, why:"Renal cells take up and concentrate solutes."},
  {t:"Renal transport, accumulation and metabolism within the kidney cells", correct:true, why:"Compounds pass through, accumulate in and are metabolized by nephron cells."},
  {t:"Sensitivity to vasoconstrictors acting on the afferent and efferent arterioles", correct:true, why:"Reduced blood flow reduces GFR and changes what the kidney receives."},
  {t:"A barrier as tight as the blood–brain barrier", correct:false, why:"The kidney has no such barrier; it is exposed to everything in the blood."},
  {t:"Resident Kupffer cells", correct:false, why:"Kupffer cells belong to the liver."}],
 teach:"The kidney is susceptible for reasons parallel to the liver: it receives a large delivery of potential toxins in the systemic circulation, including metabolites the liver has just made; its urine-concentrating processes concentrate poisons in the tubule and promote their uptake into tubular cells; and renal transport, accumulation and metabolism make in-situ toxicity likely. Its unique feature is sensitivity to endogenous vasoconstrictors acting on the afferent and efferent arterioles.",
 quote:"It gets lots of blood flowing to it, especially blood that has gone through the liver and maybe lots of metabolites have been created by the liver and they're heading to the kidney. Um, it, like the liver, the cells in the kidney often will do things like uptake compounds into themselves, so they're concentrating solutes, compounds into the cells themselves ... and then they can also be metabolized by the kidney cells.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Nephrotoxicity - Why are kidneys susceptible?’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-031", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"nephro", skill:"apply",
 concept:"kidney-vasoconstrictors", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is the consequence of vasoconstriction of the afferent and efferent arterioles for the kidney?",
 options:[
  {t:"Reduced renal blood flow and a fall in GFR", correct:true, why:"Her unique-to-the-kidney point: constrict the arterioles and the glomerular filtration rate drops, and the concentration of what the kidney receives changes."},
  {t:"Increased GFR from the higher pressure", correct:false, why:"Reduced flow lowers GFR."},
  {t:"No effect, as the kidney autoregulates completely", correct:false, why:"Sensitivity to vasoconstrictors is listed precisely as a vulnerability."},
  {t:"Loss of the urine-concentrating mechanism", correct:false, why:"That mechanism is a separate reason for susceptibility."},
  {t:"Interstitial nephritis", correct:false, why:"Interstitial nephritis is an inflammatory, often allergic injury, not a flow effect."}],
 teach:"The kidney is sensitive to endogenous vasoconstrictors. Anything that constricts the afferent or efferent arterioles reduces blood flow to the glomerulus and reduces GFR, and vasoconstriction or vasodilation of those arterioles also changes the concentration of solutes the kidney receives. Prerenal injury and vasoconstriction are among the mechanisms of chemically induced acute renal failure in the table.",
 quote:"it is sensitive to. Uh, any vasoconstriction that will go on with the arterials, both the afferent and the efferent arterial, if you reduce that blood flow, you're going to reduce GFR",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Nephrotoxicity - Why are kidneys susceptible?’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-032", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"nephro", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"renal-injury-types", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following are mechanisms of chemically induced acute renal failure in the table of site-specific nephrotoxicity? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Vasoconstriction", correct:true, why:"The category she named for NSAIDs; radiocontrast agents, cyclosporine and tacrolimus are other listed examples. The table also has a separate prerenal column (diuretics, angiotensin receptor antagonists, ACE inhibitors, antihypertensives)."},
  {t:"Crystalluria", correct:true, why:"Sulfonamides, methotrexate, acyclovir, triamterene, ethylene glycol, protease inhibitors."},
  {t:"Tubular toxicity", correct:true, why:"Aminoglycosides, cisplatin, vancomycin, pentamidine, radiocontrast agents, heavy metals and others."},
  {t:"Interstitial nephritis", correct:true, why:"Antibiotics, NSAIDs, diuretics; she adds the proton pump inhibitors."},
  {t:"Canalicular cholestasis", correct:false, why:"That is a liver response."},
  {t:"Myelinopathy", correct:false, why:"That is a pattern of neurotoxicity."}],
 teach:"The table sorts drug-induced acute renal failure by the mechanism of damage: prerenal, vasoconstriction, crystalluria, tubular toxicity, endothelial injury, glomerulopathy and interstitial nephritis. As in the liver, a nephrotoxin usually causes a discrete kind of injury, so each column has its own classic compounds (aminoglycosides and cisplatin under tubular toxicity, sulfonamides and acyclovir under crystalluria). Canalicular cholestasis and myelinopathy are not renal categories; they belong to the liver and the nervous system.",
 quote:"do you need to memorize this table? No, what you need to know are these different kinds of renal damage just in general, and we will talk about these again later in my time with you",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Site-Specific Nephrotoxicity’ (Klaassen Table 14-2); transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-033", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"nephro", skill:"drug",
 concept:"nsaid-renal-injury", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which commonly used drug class did she single out on the renal injury table as causing three kinds of injury (interstitial nephritis, vasoconstriction and glomerulopathy), and what is the advice to patients taking it?",
 options:[
  {t:"NSAIDs; limit their use and drink plenty of water", correct:true, why:"The class she worries about most: a common medication that can cause three kinds of renal injury."},
  {t:"Aminoglycosides; take them with food", correct:false, why:"Aminoglycosides sit under tubular toxicity."},
  {t:"Proton pump inhibitors; take them for at least a month", correct:false, why:"PPIs cause interstitial nephritis, and the OTC label limits use to two weeks."},
  {t:"Sulfonamides; avoid water", correct:false, why:"Sulfonamides cause crystalluria; more water, not less, is the general advice."},
  {t:"Heavy metals; limit use to two weeks", correct:false, why:"Heavy metals are under tubular toxicity and have no use to limit."}],
 teach:"Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) are the drugs on the table she worries about most, because they are so commonly used: she named interstitial nephritis, vasoconstriction and glomerulopathy. Her counsel is to limit their use and drink lots of water while taking them.",
 quote:"One of the things I worry about the most are our NSAIDs. They can cause interstitial nephritis. They can cause vasoconstriction. So, our NSAIDs, they can also cause glomerular neuropathy. ... they should try to limit their use and drink lots of water while they're taking them, OK?",
 note:"\"glomerular neuropathy\" in the transcript is the table's glomerulopathy.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Site-Specific Nephrotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-034", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"nephro", skill:"apply",
 concept:"ppi-interstitial-nephritis", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A geriatric patient who is allergic to aspirin starts over-the-counter omeprazole for reflux. Months later his serum creatinine is high. Which kidney injury is this, and what should be done?",
 options:[
  {t:"Interstitial nephritis, an allergic response to the PPI; stop it and never restart", correct:true, why:"Her father's case: PPIs cause interstitial nephritis, especially in patients with an aspirin allergy; the allergy shows up in the kidney rather than as hives."},
  {t:"Crystalluria; increase fluids and continue", correct:false, why:"PPIs do not cause crystals, and the drug had to be stopped for good."},
  {t:"Tubular toxicity from drug concentrated in the tubule; reduce the dose and continue the PPI", correct:false, why:"The mechanism was immunologic, not concentration."},
  {t:"Prerenal injury; add an ACE inhibitor", correct:false, why:"Not a flow problem, and ACE inhibitors are themselves in the table's prerenal column."},
  {t:"Glomerulopathy from aspirin; stop the aspirin", correct:false, why:"He was not taking aspirin; the aspirin allergy predisposed him to react to the PPI."}],
 teach:"Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) such as omeprazole are not on the table but belong under interstitial nephritis. Her father, a geriatric patient allergic to aspirin, took OTC omeprazole for several months while his physical was delayed; the routine blood work showed a high serum creatinine. The literature (then coming out of Australia) showed PPIs cause interstitial nephritis, especially with an aspirin allergy: an allergic response that appears as kidney injury rather than hives or breathing trouble. He stopped the PPI for good and needed erythropoietin for a while.",
 quote:"I found that PPIs can cause interstitial nephritis too, especially in patients that have an aspirin allergy. ... he was having basically. An allergic response to the PPI just like aspirin, and that allergic response is manifesting as interstitial nephritis in some patients",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Site-Specific Nephrotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-035", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"nephro", skill:"recall",
 concept:"otc-ppi-two-weeks", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"The label of an over-the-counter proton pump inhibitor says to limit use to two weeks. Which pair gives the two reasons?",
 options:[
  {t:"Reflux that needs more than two weeks should be checked, and PPIs can cause interstitial nephritis", correct:true, why:"Both reasons in her words: get the GERD checked out, and the kidney risk."},
  {t:"Tolerance develops after two weeks, and the tablets lose their potency after two weeks of exposure to air", correct:false, why:"Neither tolerance nor potency is involved; the limit exists because reflux needing a PPI that long should be checked, and because PPIs can cause interstitial nephritis."},
  {t:"The drug accumulates in zone 3 of the liver, and it blocks the bile ducts", correct:false, why:"PPIs were not linked to liver injury; the second reason for the limit is a kidney risk, interstitial nephritis."},
  {t:"It causes crystalluria, and it raises blood pressure", correct:false, why:"PPIs were placed under interstitial nephritis, not crystalluria."},
  {t:"It is only effective for two weeks", correct:false, why:"The limit is not about the drug stopping working; it is a prompt to get persistent reflux checked out and to avoid the kidney risk of longer use."}],
 teach:"The two-week limit is a prompt to stop and check. Reflux bad enough to need a PPI for longer should be evaluated, and longer use risks interstitial nephritis, which does not show itself the way an allergy with hives does. In her father's case it appeared only as a high serum creatinine on routine blood work after a few months of OTC omeprazole, by which time he needed erythropoietin for a while.",
 quote:"It says limit your use to two weeks. If you need to use it for longer, you should consult a doctor, OK? There's a couple of reasons for that. One reason is if you have reflux that's bad enough to need a PPI for more than 2 weeks, you probably should get it checked out ... And another reason is the fact that these drugs can cause interstitial nephritis, OK?",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Site-Specific Nephrotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-036", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"nephro", skill:"recall",
 concept:"site-specific-nephro-reasons", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Several site-specific differences explain why toxins injure particular parts of the nephron. Which one did she single out as probably the most important?",
 options:[
  {t:"Which metabolizing enzymes are expressed in which part of the kidney", correct:true, why:"Metabolism inside renal cells, varying along the nephron, sets where in-situ toxicity occurs."},
  {t:"Regional differences in blood flow", correct:false, why:"Blood flow does differ along the nephron and is on the slide's list, but she ranked metabolism first: which enzymes each part of the kidney expresses decides where a toxic metabolite is made."},
  {t:"Cellular energetics", correct:false, why:"Energetics is on the list, but it was not her lead reason; injury localizes mainly where the renal cells' enzymes metabolize the compound."},
  {t:"Regenerative and repair mechanisms", correct:false, why:"Repair mechanisms are on the list, but she put enzyme expression first, since that sets where in the nephron the toxic metabolism happens."},
  {t:"The physicochemical properties of the epithelium lining each segment of the nephron", correct:false, why:"Epithelial properties are on the list, but she ranked metabolism by locally expressed enzymes as probably the most important; she explained tubular toxicity and crystalluria by the chemistry of the drugs, not the epithelium."}],
 teach:"Many factors vary along the nephron: blood flow, transport and accumulation, the epithelium, target reactivity, bioactivation versus detoxification, energetics and repair. She put metabolism first: renal cells metabolize the compounds they take up, and which enzymes are expressed in which part of the kidney decides where a toxic metabolite is made, so that is where injury appears, as CYP2E1 places acetaminophen injury in zone 3 of the liver. Tubular toxicity and crystalluria depend more on the drug's own chemistry.",
 quote:"probably the most important reason is that you'll have all that metabolism going on in the renal cells, and it will depend on what enzymes are expressed in what part of the cell or what part of the kidney to, to contribute to that site specificity ... And then the other types of injury like the, the, um, the tubular toxicity, the crystal urea, that has to deal more with the chemistry of those drugs, OK?",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Reasons for Site-Specific Nephrotoxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

/* ---------------- Idiosyncratic and immunologic responses ---------------- */
{id:"L15-037", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"immune", skill:"recall",
 concept:"idiosyncratic-definition", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which description fits an idiosyncratic toxic response?",
 options:[
  {t:"A genetically determined, abnormally strong reaction in one person, not yet explained", correct:true, why:"Qualitatively like the response in others but extreme, and genetically determined; pharmacogenomics is starting to explain these."},
  {t:"Any reaction that occurs with low frequency in the population, whatever its mechanism or cause", correct:false, why:"That is the improper use of the term flagged on the slide."},
  {t:"A hypersensitivity reaction of type 1 through 4", correct:false, why:"That is an immunologic response."},
  {t:"A reaction that occurs in everyone at a high enough dose", correct:false, why:"That is ordinary dose-related toxicity."},
  {t:"A reaction confined to the kidney", correct:false, why:"The liver, not the kidney, is the organ most prone to idiosyncratic reactions."}],
 teach:"Idiosyncratic toxicity is a genetically determined abnormal reactivity to a chemical: the response is qualitatively like other people's but extreme, strong and unexpected, and could not be explained. Using the word for every low-frequency reaction is improper. As pharmacogenomics advances, some idiosyncratic reactions become explained reactions, traced to a rare genetic variant.",
 quote:"idiosyncratic means we, it's a reaction that we don't know why it's happening always, and often idiosyncratic reactions are pretty strong. And sometimes unexpected.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Idiosyncratic Toxicity’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-038", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"immune", skill:"recall",
 concept:"immunologic-two-categories", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Drug-triggered immune responses (allergic responses, a heading better read as immunologic responses) fall into which two broad categories?",
 options:[
  {t:"Hypersensitivity and autoimmune reactions", correct:true, why:"Hypersensitivity is the allergic response (types 1 through 4); an autoimmune reaction is the immune system turning on the patient's own body."},
  {t:"Idiosyncratic and organ-specific reactions", correct:false, why:"Those are other categories of toxic endpoint."},
  {t:"Hapten formation and direct activation", correct:false, why:"Those are two ways a drug can be recognized as foreign, not the two categories of response."},
  {t:"Local and systemic reactions", correct:false, why:"Local versus systemic is a general-concepts distinction."},
  {t:"Immediate and delayed reactions", correct:false, why:"Timing is a general-concepts distinction."}],
 teach:"Drugs may be recognized as foreign and provoke an immune response. The two main categories are hypersensitivity (allergic reactions of types 1, 2, 3 and 4, covered previously with Dr. Medina) and autoimmune reactions, in which the immune system fights the patient's own body. She asked the class to change the slide title from allergic to immunologic responses, as the more accurate term.",
 quote:"why don't you change allergic to immunologic responses, OK? ... So hypersensitivity is the first category. So that would be like an allergic response, like a type 1 or a type 2, type 3, or type 4 type of allergic response ... And then we see autoimmune reactions occurring.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Allergic Responses’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-039", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"immune", skill:"apply",
 concept:"hapten", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"How does a small-molecule drug (under 600 daltons) trigger an immune response?",
 options:[
  {t:"It binds to a body protein, and the drug–protein complex acts as a hapten", correct:true, why:"The complex is what the immune system responds to."},
  {t:"It activates the immune system directly, the way a therapeutic protein or peptide does", correct:false, why:"Direct activation is how large drugs, peptides and proteins, do it."},
  {t:"It is too small to be seen by the immune system at all", correct:false, why:"Small molecules can be seen once they are bound as haptens."},
  {t:"It crosses the blood–brain barrier and activates glial cells", correct:false, why:"The barrier has nothing to do with it."},
  {t:"It is concentrated by the kidney tubule", correct:false, why:"Renal concentration is a nephrotoxicity mechanism."}],
 teach:"Both small and large drugs can provoke an immune response. Small molecules (less than 600 daltons) may bind to certain body proteins, creating a hapten, a drug–protein complex that the immune system recognizes and mounts a response against. Large drugs, therapeutic peptides and proteins, may activate the immune system directly.",
 quote:"those small drugs, they may sometimes get bound up by certain proteins in the body, creating haptins, and that drug protein complex called a haptin can trigger the immune system to respond to it, OK?",
 note:"The transcript renders hapten as \"haptin\". The 600-dalton figure is on the slide.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Allergic Responses’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-040", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"immune", skill:"apply",
 concept:"biologicals-autoimmune", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which group of therapies is increasingly triggering autoimmune responses as more of them reach the market?",
 options:[
  {t:"Biologicals: protein and peptide therapies", correct:true, why:"Large drugs activate the immune system directly, and the resulting autoimmune responses are hard to predict and often bad."},
  {t:"Small-molecule NSAIDs taken for more than two weeks", correct:false, why:"NSAIDs are a kidney story here."},
  {t:"Inhaled halogen gases", correct:false, why:"Those are caustic pulmonary toxins."},
  {t:"Proton pump inhibitors", correct:false, why:"PPIs were her interstitial nephritis example."},
  {t:"Organophosphates", correct:false, why:"Those disturb neurotransmission."}],
 teach:"Autoimmune reactions to medications are happening more and more often as biologicals, protein and peptide therapies, come onto the market. These responses do not happen to everyone, are hard to predict and can be quite harmful; the therapeutics courses will pair specific biologicals with the autoimmune responses they can trigger.",
 quote:"That is happening with more and more frequency as we continue to put on the market our biologicals. So, our, our proteins, our peptide therapies are often the kinds of medications that will trigger the immune system to cause an autoimmune response, OK?",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Allergic Responses’; transcript 10/14"},

/* ---------------- Her Poll Everywhere review questions (verbatim) ---------------- */
{id:"L15-041", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"polls", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"poll-general-concepts-tf", tags:["smith","pollev"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statements are true regarding toxic effects? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"A delayed response to a toxin may be observed weeks to years after exposure.", correct:true, why:"Her answer: yes. DES and TOCP are the extreme examples."},
  {t:"Tissue injury from a toxic exposure is always reversible.", correct:false, why:"Her answer: no. Reversibility depends on the tissue's ability to regenerate; CNS injury, cancer and birth defects are irreversible."},
  {t:"Toxins will not cause local effects.", correct:false, why:"Not true: many exposures cause an effect right where the body is first touched by them."},
  {t:"Systemic effects do not require absorption from the site of contact.", correct:false, why:"To become systemic a substance must enter the bloodstream, so it has to be absorbed."},
  {t:"It is possible to have immediate effects with each dose in a chronic exposure scenario.", correct:true, why:"Yes: the worker who gets drowsy or a headache every day from the same leak, with no tolerance."}],
 teach:"A delayed effect appears after time has lapsed, sometimes years (DES and vaginal cancer). Injury is reversible only where tissue can regenerate: the liver usually recovers, but CNS damage, cancer and birth defects are irreversible. Local effects occur at the site of first contact (chlorine gas). A systemic effect means the substance travels in the bloodstream, so it must be absorbed first. Without tolerance, a chronic exposure such as a daily workplace leak can produce an immediate effect each time.",
 quote:"could a delayed response to a toxin be observed weeks to years after exposure? Yeah, yeah, OK. Uh, how about B? Tissue injury from a toxic exposure is always reversible. No ... Systemic effects do not require absorption from the site of contact. ... It's got to be absorbed, OK? So, D is not correct. ... Is it possible to have immediate effects with each dose in a chronic exposure scenario? Yeah",
 note:"\"Is it allowing you to choose more than one? Gosh, dang it, every time I do this, I can't get it to work right. OK, well, choose one.\" The five statements are from the PollEV slide.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf PollEV slide ‘Which statements are true regarding toxic effects’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-042", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"polls", skill:"tell",
 concept:"poll-non-organ-specific", tags:["smith","pollev"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which is an non-organ specific toxic response?",
 options:[
  {t:"Neurotoxicity", correct:false, why:"The nerves: organ-specific."},
  {t:"Pulmonary toxicity", correct:false, why:"The lungs: organ-specific."},
  {t:"Teratogenicity", correct:true, why:"Her answer: teratogenicity is the one on the list that is non-organ specific."},
  {t:"Nephrotoxicity", correct:false, why:"The kidneys: organ-specific."}],
 teach:"Each organ-specific response is named for the organ it injures: neurotoxicity the nerves, pulmonary toxicity the lungs, nephrotoxicity the kidneys. Teratogenesis is defined by its outcome, the induction of birth defects in the fetus, and for a given teratogen the time of exposure during gestation changes the type of effect, so it is grouped with fibrosis, genotoxicity and cancer as non-organ-specific.",
 quote:"neurotoxicity is the nerves. Pulmonary, pulmonary toxins in the lungs, nephrotoxins the kidneys. So teratogenicity is the one on this list that is non-organ specific, OK?",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf PollEV slide ‘Which is an non-organ specific toxic response?’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-043", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"polls", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"poll-liver-susceptible", tags:["smith","pollev"], source:"both",
 stem:"The liver is very susceptible to drug toxicity because… Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"It expresses many enzymes that may metabolize drugs to toxic compounds", correct:true, why:"The liver does a lot of metabolism, and its enzymes may bioactivate a drug to a hepatotoxic metabolite right there in the liver."},
  {t:"It receives a large volume of blood via the portal circulation", correct:true, why:"Oral exposures are carried by the portal circulation straight to the liver, so it is exposed to a lot of what is ingested."},
  {t:"It is a large organ", correct:false, why:"Size is not part of the mechanism; the liver is susceptible because of what it does: it takes up, concentrates and metabolizes compounds and hosts its own immune cells."},
  {t:"It is able to uptake and concentrate xenobiotics", correct:true, why:"Its job is to take up and concentrate compounds out of the blood, so toxicants build up inside liver cells."},
  {t:"It has its own immune cells to mount a quick inflammatory response", correct:true, why:"Kupffer cells cleaning up damaged cells release inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species, which add to the injury from a hepatotoxin."}],
 teach:"Each correct reason is a step in how a toxin reaches and harms the liver. Oral compounds go straight to the liver in the portal blood; the liver takes them up and concentrates them in its cells; its many enzymes may bioactivate them to toxic metabolites right there; and when cells are damaged, Kupffer cells cleaning up release inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species that add to the injury. Size is not part of that chain.",
 quote:"it expresses many enzymes that may metabolize drug to toxic compounds. That's right. It receives it receives a large volume of blood via the portal circulation. Yes, that is true. It's a large organ, yeah, that really doesn't have anything to do with it, right? ... all of these were correct except for the fact that it's a large organ",
 note:"Reading option D aloud she said \"uptake and concentrate antibiotics or other, or compounds\"; the slide says xenobiotics.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf PollEV slide ‘The liver is very susceptible to drug toxicity because…’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-044", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"polls", skill:"recall",
 concept:"poll-regional-liver", tags:["smith","pollev"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statement is one reason for regional susceptibility to toxicity across the liver?",
 options:[
  {t:"Relatively high oxygen content in Zone 1", correct:true, why:"Her answer: zone 1 is the high-oxygen zone, and enzymes that need oxygen to do their metabolism may make the hepatotoxic metabolite there."},
  {t:"Homogenous expression of P450s across the zones", correct:false, why:"No: CYP2E1 is the example of an enzyme expressed in a particular zone; most metabolizing enzymes are not homogeneous across the liver."},
  {t:"Zone 2 has few Kupfer cells", correct:false, why:"Not true: Kupffer cells go all over the liver, not just one area."}],
 teach:"Blood enters zone 1 well oxygenated and loses oxygen to the hepatocytes on its way to zone 3. If the enzymes that metabolize a toxin need oxygen, the high oxygen in zone 1 helps them work there, and if their product is hepatotoxic, zone 1 is injured. The other source of regional injury is uneven enzyme expression (CYP2E1 highest in zone 3). Kupffer cells move all over the liver, so their distribution does not create a regional pattern.",
 quote:"Do you have high oxygen content in zone one? Is it zone 1 or zone 3? Zone 1, right? OK. Yes, and if the enzymes that do metabolism there, uh, need oxygen to do their work, having a lot of oxygen right there is helpful to those enzymes to do their metabolism, and unfortunately that metabolism may result in the metabolite that's, that's hepatotoxic.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf PollEV slide ‘Which statement is one reason for regional susceptibility to toxicity across the liver?’; transcript 10/14"},

/* ---------------- Overdose changes pharmacokinetics ---------------- */
{id:"L15-045", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"odkin", skill:"apply",
 concept:"large-vd-hemodialysis", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient who overdosed on a drug with a very large volume of distribution gets a standard round of hemodialysis, yet the blood concentration is still high afterward. Why?",
 options:[
  {t:"Most of the drug is sequestered in lipid, muscle or bone and must move back into the blood before dialysis can reach it", correct:true, why:"Dialysis only clears what is in the blood; the reservoir in the tissues refills it, so dialysis has to run a long time."},
  {t:"Hemodialysis cannot remove any drug from the plasma of a poisoned patient whose kidneys are still working normally and whose blood pressure is stable", correct:false, why:"Dialysis does clear the plasma; the problem is the inaccessible reservoir."},
  {t:"The drug is bound to charcoal in the gut", correct:false, why:"Charcoal was not given; this is a distribution problem."},
  {t:"Its half-life became shorter in overdose", correct:false, why:"Overdose tends to lengthen half-life, not shorten it."},
  {t:"The dialysis machine is at the wrong target of action", correct:false, why:"A student's guess; the drug is spread throughout the body, not at one target."}],
 teach:"A large volume of distribution means the drug is not staying in the vasculature; it is distributed and sequestered in lipids, muscle or bone. In a big overdose a huge amount sits in those sites. Hemodialysis pulls drug out of the blood, but then the clinician has to wait for more to move out of the tissues before dialysis can grab it again, so patients overdosed on large-Vd drugs need prolonged dialysis. The slide lists large-Vd drugs (over 5 L/kg): antidepressants, antipsychotics, antimalarials, opioids, propranolol and verapamil.",
 quote:"you have to wait for the drug to move out of the lipids, or out of the muscle or out of the bone back into the blood before hemodialysis is going to grab it again. So these people that have overdosed on drugs with very large volumes of distribution, you probably are going to have to do hemodialysis for a long time",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘How Pharmacokinetics change in Overdose’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-046", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"odkin", skill:"apply",
 concept:"saturated-first-pass", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"An oral overdose of a drug that normally undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism produces much higher blood concentrations than expected. Why?",
 options:[
  {t:"Liver metabolism is saturated, so the first-pass effect falls and more drug enters the circulation", correct:true, why:"Too much drug for the liver to handle goes right through unmetabolized."},
  {t:"The drug is absorbed directly into the systemic circulation, bypassing the portal vein and the liver entirely", correct:false, why:"Oral drug still reaches the liver first; the liver just cannot keep up."},
  {t:"The liver bioactivates more of it", correct:false, why:"The point is less metabolism, not more."},
  {t:"Protein binding increases in overdose", correct:false, why:"Protein binding capacity is exceeded, which raises free drug for a different reason."},
  {t:"Gastric emptying speeds up", correct:false, why:"Overdose tends to slow dissolution and gastric emptying."}],
 teach:"Clearance changes in overdose. Normally an oral dose is partly cleared by first pass in the liver. Send the liver a huge amount and it does all the metabolism it can, but the excess passes through unmetabolized into the circulation, so blood concentrations are higher than expected even for a drug with a large first-pass effect.",
 quote:"If you send to the liver a huge amount of that drug, and the liver's doing all the work it can to metabolize it and help you clear it, but you've got too much drug for the liver to handle. Then that drug is going to go just right through the liver. It's not going to be metabolized, and it's gonna go into circulation",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘How Pharmacokinetics change in Overdose’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-047", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"odkin", skill:"apply",
 concept:"iron-gut-injury-absorption", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Oral iron is normally absorbed by a saturable active transport, so a couple of tablets deliver only so much. Why does an iron overdose produce very high absorption?",
 options:[
  {t:"Iron is caustic and damages the gut, removing the protection of saturable transport", correct:true, why:"With the gut wall blown apart, dissolved iron goes straight into the bloodstream."},
  {t:"The transporters are induced by the large dose", correct:false, why:"Induction is not the mechanism; tissue damage is."},
  {t:"Iron becomes lipophilic at high concentrations and diffuses across the gut wall on its own", correct:false, why:"The chemistry does not change; the barrier is destroyed."},
  {t:"Iron is absorbed by the lungs instead", correct:false, why:"The exposure is oral."},
  {t:"Iron chelates the charcoal given to treat it", correct:false, why:"Charcoal plays no part; the high absorption comes from iron's caustic damage to the gut wall, which removes the saturable active transport that normally limits uptake."}],
 teach:"Much gut absorption is by active transport, not diffusion, and those transporters saturate: with iron, that saturation is protective, since free iron in the blood is very damaging. In overdose iron is so caustic that it damages the gut wall, and the saturable barrier is gone: dissolving iron passes straight into the bloodstream. This is the slide's point that drugs may injure the gastrointestinal tract, increasing absorption.",
 quote:"people that overdose on iron, iron is very caustic, it actually blows apart your gut. Therefore, you don't have the protection of a saturable active transport. What happens? You get iron going straight from the gut, it's dissolving in there, and then whoop, it's just getting right into the bloodstream",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘How Pharmacokinetics change in Overdose’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-048", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"odkin", skill:"apply",
 concept:"protein-binding-exceeded", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A highly protein-bound drug is taken in overdose. What happens to the fraction of free drug, and why does it matter?",
 options:[
  {t:"It rises, as plasma protein binding capacity is exceeded, and free drug is what causes toxicity", correct:true, why:"The plasma proteins are saturated, so more drug circulates unbound."},
  {t:"It falls, as more protein is made in response", correct:false, why:"The plasma proteins do not rise to meet the dose; a big dose saturates them, so the free fraction rises, not falls."},
  {t:"It is unchanged, as binding is a fixed percentage", correct:false, why:"The percentage holds only while binding capacity is not exceeded."},
  {t:"It rises, as the drug is concentrated in zone 3 of the liver and released from there into the plasma", correct:false, why:"The direction is right but the reason is wrong: free drug rises because the plasma proteins in the blood are saturated, not because of anything in the liver."},
  {t:"It falls, so the overdose is less toxic", correct:false, why:"The reverse: more free drug, more toxicity."}],
 teach:"Plasma protein binding has a capacity. A big dose of a protein-bound drug saturates the plasma proteins, leaving a larger fraction free in the blood, and free drug is the fraction that acts, so the risk of toxicity rises. She qualified the slide's wording: it means blood protein binding.",
 quote:"If a medication gets blood protein bound and you take a big dose. Um, there's going to be more drug free in the ... blood, not bound up to the plasma proteins. ... Therefore, you've got a risk for having toxicity because a lot of that drug is free. You've oversaturated those plasma proteins.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘How Pharmacokinetics change in Overdose’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-049", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"odkin", skill:"recall",
 concept:"zero-order-in-overdose", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Most drugs are eliminated by first-order kinetics. At the very high plasma concentrations of an overdose, elimination may change to:",
 options:[
  {t:"Zero order, extending the half-life", correct:true, why:"The clearance bullets on the slide: high plasma concentrations may change elimination to zero order."},
  {t:"Second order, shortening the half-life", correct:false, why:"The slide describes a change from first to zero order, and its effect is to extend the half-life, not shorten it."},
  {t:"First order with a shorter half-life", correct:false, why:"The change is away from first order and lengthens half-life."},
  {t:"Renal elimination only", correct:false, why:"Route is not the change described."},
  {t:"No elimination at all", correct:false, why:"Elimination does not stop; the slide says it may change from first order to zero order, which extends the half-life."}],
 teach:"Most drugs are eliminated by first-order kinetics. The slide's last clearance bullet says the very high plasma concentrations of an overdose may change elimination to zero order, which extends the half-life, so the drug stays in the body longer than its usual half-life would predict. Like saturated first-pass metabolism and exceeded protein binding, it is one way an overdose keeps more drug around for longer.",
 note:"She displayed this bullet and asked for questions on the clearance list but did not restate it aloud; keyed from the slide.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘How Pharmacokinetics change in Overdose’"},

/* ---------------- Overdose changes pharmacodynamics ---------------- */
{id:"L15-050", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"odpd", skill:"figure",
 concept:"drug-a-vs-b-overdose", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the Katzung figure, drugs A and B share a mechanism and produce sedation at the same dose. What does the figure show about them in overdose?",
 options:[
  {t:"Alike at the therapeutic endpoint, but in overdose their therapeutic indices differ and B can be pushed higher without an unwanted effect", correct:true, why:"Her answer: B. Increasing the dose of A leads to coma; B plateaus before coma."},
  {t:"They behave identically at every dose, from sedation through hypnosis and anesthesia to coma, as they share a mechanism and a therapeutic dose", correct:false, why:"They look the same at the therapeutic endpoint but differ in overdose: only A reaches coma on the figure."},
  {t:"A is safer, as it reaches coma only at the highest dose", correct:false, why:"A has the linear response that reaches coma; that makes it the more dangerous drug in overdose."},
  {t:"Neither can be dosed above sedation", correct:false, why:"B can be given in higher doses without an unwanted effect."},
  {t:"The figure cannot distinguish them", correct:false, why:"The plateau of B against the straight rise of A is the point of the figure."}],
 teach:"Katzung's figure plots CNS effect (sedation, hypnosis, anesthesia, coma) against increasing dose for two drugs with the same mechanism. Both sedate at the same dose, so at the therapeutic endpoint they look alike. In overdose their outcomes differ: A rises in a straight line to coma, B levels off at anesthesia. One drug is therefore more dangerous in overdose than the other on the basis of its pharmacodynamics; the overlap of the therapeutic and toxic response curves, the idea behind the therapeutic index, is what matters.",
 quote:"Which one can you increase the dose and not have ... an unwanted adverse effect? B. ... So, drug A, if you increase the dose more, you're getting to an unwanted effect, OK?",
 note:"The slide says two medications may have the same therapeutic index but unequal safe dosing; in speech she said both that A and B have \"the same therapeutic index\" and that B's \"therapeutic index is different than drug A\". The figure reading (B plateaus, A reaches coma) is what she keyed.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘How Pharmacodynamics change in Overdose’ (Katzung figure); transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-051", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"odpd", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tca-overdose-double-whammy", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why is a tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) overdose so dangerous from a pharmacodynamic point of view?",
 options:[
  {t:"It reduces sweating while increasing muscle activity and the risk of seizures", correct:true, why:"Two effects that are not part of therapy combine: heat builds from muscle activity and seizures, and the body cannot shed it by sweating."},
  {t:"It is cleared by zero-order kinetics", correct:false, why:"That is a kinetic change, not the pharmacodynamic one she described."},
  {t:"It has a very small volume of distribution", correct:false, why:"Antidepressants have a large Vd on the kinetics slide."},
  {t:"It reduces blood flow to the kidney and liver, so the drug is eliminated more slowly", correct:false, why:"That is the beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker example."},
  {t:"It binds activated charcoal irreversibly", correct:false, why:"Charcoal binding concerns preventing absorption, not pharmacodynamics; the TCA danger is reduced sweating combined with more muscle activity and seizures, so the heat cannot be shed."}],
 teach:"At a toxic dose a drug can add pharmacodynamic effects that are absent at therapeutic doses. TCAs reduce sweating while increasing muscular activity and the potential for seizures: a double whammy, since sweating would help shed the heat the seizures generate. TCA overdoses are horrible to treat, and it is rare to save the patient.",
 quote:"the TCAs, they reduce sweating. In an overdose situation, that's one of their pharmacodynamic effects. At the same time that they are increasing muscular activity and potential for seizures ... so it's a double whammy in an overdose situation",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘How Pharmacodynamics change in Overdose’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-052", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"odpd", skill:"apply",
 concept:"bb-ccb-reduce-elimination", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In a beta-blocker or calcium channel blocker overdose, what pharmacodynamic effect beyond the cardiac one keeps the dose high?",
 options:[
  {t:"Reduced blood flow to the kidney and liver, so the drug is eliminated more slowly", correct:true, why:"The organs of elimination are underperfused, clearance slows, and the concentration stays up."},
  {t:"Increased sweating that concentrates the drug in the plasma by removing water from the circulation", correct:false, why:"Sweating is the TCA example, in the other direction."},
  {t:"Saturation of plasma protein binding", correct:false, why:"That is a kinetic change, not this pharmacodynamic one."},
  {t:"Induction of CYP2E1", correct:false, why:"Enzyme induction plays no part; these drugs keep their own level high by reducing blood flow to the kidneys and liver, the organs of elimination."},
  {t:"Bioactivation to a cardiotoxic metabolite", correct:false, why:"No toxic metabolite is involved; the cardiac effect is compounded by reduced blood flow to the kidneys and liver, so less drug is eliminated and the dose stays high."}],
 teach:"Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers are used for blood pressure and heart rate. In overdose they alter cardiac function, and they also reduce blood flow to the kidneys and the liver, the organs of elimination. The body therefore cannot clear the drug well, the dose stays high, and the kinetic and pharmacodynamic changes feed on each other.",
 quote:"Reducing blood flow to the kidneys and the liver, you're reducing the body's ability to eliminate the drug. ... you're also reducing blood flow to your organs of elimination, OK? And therefore you can't eliminate the drugs very well, and the dose stays high.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘How Pharmacodynamics change in Overdose’; transcript 10/14"},

/* ---------------- Three angles; four toxicokinetic methods ---------------- */
{id:"L15-053", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"approach", skill:"recall",
 concept:"three-treatment-angles", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The three basic approaches to treating a poisoned patient are:",
 options:[
  {t:"Toxicokinetic-based, inactivation of the poison, and pharmacologically based", correct:true, why:"Treat the kinetics, inactivate the poison, or counter its pharmacodynamics."},
  {t:"Emesis, gastric lavage and activated charcoal", correct:false, why:"Those are three methods within one toxicokinetic approach, preventing absorption."},
  {t:"Prevent absorption, inhibit bioactivation, enhance metabolism, enhance elimination", correct:false, why:"The four toxicokinetic methods, not the three approaches."},
  {t:"Antagonist, agonist and antidote", correct:false, why:"Antagonists and agonists are pharmacological categories within one approach."},
  {t:"Hemodialysis, chelation and antivenom", correct:false, why:"Examples from different approaches, not the approaches themselves."}],
 teach:"Treatment of a poisoning or overdose comes from one of three angles: toxicokinetic-based treatment (change what happens to the poison in the body), inactivation of the poison, or pharmacologically based treatment (counter what the poison does). The toxicokinetic approach has four methods and the pharmacological approach four categories; inactivation covers chelators and antivenoms, which are scheduled for 10/19.",
 quote:"We will either think about treating them based on kinetics, or we'll think about treating them based on trying to inactivate the poison. Or we will think about trying to treat them based on, basically the pharmacology, the pharmacodynamics of what's going on, OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘General principles for treating the poisoned patient’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-054", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"approach", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"four-toxicokinetic-methods", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following are toxicokinetic-based methods of treating a poisoning? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Prevention of absorption", correct:true, why:"Emesis, lavage, whole bowel irrigation, activated charcoal."},
  {t:"Inhibition of toxication (bioactivation)", correct:true, why:"Stop the poison being converted to a nastier compound."},
  {t:"Enhancement of metabolism (detoxification)", correct:true, why:"Speed the conversion to something less harmful."},
  {t:"Enhancement of elimination", correct:true, why:"Get the poison out faster."},
  {t:"Giving a receptor antagonist", correct:false, why:"That is a pharmacologically based treatment."},
  {t:"Binding the poison with a chelator", correct:false, why:"That is inactivation of the poison."}],
 teach:"Toxicokinetic treatment works on what the body does with the poison, aiming to decrease the amount of poison in the body and so the potential for end-organ damage. Each method targets one step: stop the poison getting in (prevent absorption), stop it being bioactivated to a nastier compound (inhibit toxication), speed its detoxification (enhance metabolism), or get it out faster (enhance elimination). Antagonists act on the poison's effects instead, and chelators inactivate it.",
 quote:"sometimes we will try to prevent absorption of the poison or the overdose drug. Sometimes we're going to try to inhibit toxication or stop the bioactivation of it to a nasty compound, OK. Sometimes we can enhance metabolism or enhance detoxification. Sometimes, OK. Sometimes we can enhance elimination. Can you see how all four of those methods are kinetically based treatments?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment’; transcript 10/14"},

/* ---------------- Preventing absorption ---------------- */
{id:"L15-055", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"absorb", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"absorption-prevention-methods", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following are methods used to prevent absorption of an ingested poison? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Emesis", correct:true, why:"Vomiting to get the compound up out of the stomach; no longer used."},
  {t:"Gastric lavage", correct:true, why:"Pumping the stomach."},
  {t:"Whole bowel irrigation", correct:true, why:"Flushing the gut as for a colonoscopy."},
  {t:"Single-dose activated charcoal", correct:true, why:"One dose, soon after ingestion."},
  {t:"Multiple-dose activated charcoal", correct:true, why:"Repeated doses for compounds that recirculate through the bile."},
  {t:"Hemodialysis", correct:false, why:"Hemodialysis enhances elimination of a poison already absorbed."},
  {t:"Sodium bicarbonate to trap a weak acid in the urine", correct:false, why:"Ion trapping enhances renal elimination; it is a 10/19 topic."}],
 teach:"Preventing absorption keeps an ingested poison from getting from the gut into the blood. Emesis brings it up from the stomach, gastric lavage pumps the stomach, whole bowel irrigation flushes the whole gut, and activated charcoal binds it so it leaves in the feces; repeated charcoal doses catch drug that the liver returns to the intestine in bile. Hemodialysis and urinary ion trapping act after absorption, so they belong to enhancing elimination.",
 quote:"Try to cause emesis, which is vomiting or barfing, right? We could try to cause emesis to get a compound up out of the stomach. We could do gastric lavage. ... How about whole bowel irrigation? ... we also could try to prevent absorption of whatever the person ingested orally using activated charcoal, and I've got two types of activated charcoal listed, well, two methods of using activated charcoal single single dose or multiple dose activated charcoal.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Prevent Absorption’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-056", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"absorb", skill:"apply",
 concept:"emesis-ipecac-aspiration", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Syrup of ipecac used to be kept in home medicine cabinets to make a child vomit after swallowing something. What is its status now, and what is the main risk of induced emesis?",
 options:[
  {t:"No longer sold in the US or used in hospitals; the biggest risk is aspiration", correct:true, why:"Vomiting can send stomach contents into the lungs."},
  {t:"Still first-line in emergency rooms for children; the main risk is dehydration from vomiting", correct:false, why:"It is no longer used; aspiration, not dehydration, is the risk she named."},
  {t:"Sold over the counter; the main risk is esophageal rupture", correct:false, why:"It is no longer sold; rupture was a lavage concern."},
  {t:"Replaced by gastric lavage as a home remedy", correct:false, why:"Lavage is a hospital procedure with its own risks."},
  {t:"Used only for extended-release tablets", correct:false, why:"Ipecac has no remaining niche use: it is no longer sold in the US or used in hospitals, because the risk of aspiration is too high."}],
 teach:"Emesis is the clearest example of a method that medicine has abandoned. Ipecac is no longer sold in the US or used in hospitals, because it is too risky: the biggest risk of emesis is aspiration. The DiPiro chapter section on general approach to treatment states that emesis is no longer used.",
 quote:"They no longer even sell it in the US. They don't even use it anymore in hospitals, OK, because it's too risky. What's the risk of emesis? Aspiration is the biggest risk.",
 note:"The transcript renders ipecac as \"Emmis Ipecac\".",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slides ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Prevent Absorption’, ‘More on preventing absorption’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-057", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"absorb", skill:"apply",
 concept:"gastric-lavage-risks", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statement about gastric lavage (pumping the stomach) is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"A tube is passed to the stomach, fluid is pumped in and sucked out; large tablets may not come back up the tube", correct:true, why:"The esophagus is not that big, so big tablets are hard to retrieve."},
  {t:"It is safe in anyone with an esophageal rupture", correct:false, why:"Esophageal rupture or stomach problems make it dangerous; it is not done."},
  {t:"The tube can safely go into the lungs", correct:false, why:"Getting the tube into the lungs, and pumping fluid into them, is one of the risks."},
  {t:"It is the same procedure as whole bowel irrigation, flushing the entire gut from the stomach to the rectum with polyethylene glycol", correct:false, why:"Irrigation flushes the whole gut from above, as for a colonoscopy."},
  {t:"It works best several hours after ingestion", correct:false, why:"Timing was given for charcoal (1 to 2 hours); lavage is risky at any time."}],
 teach:"Gastric lavage means putting a tube down to the stomach, pumping liquid in and sucking it back out. Risks: the tube can end up in the lungs and fluid can be pumped into them; the esophagus is narrow, so big tablets may not come back out through the tube; and it is dangerous in anyone with esophageal rupture or stomach problems. Experienced toxicologists may still attempt it for a massive, dangerous ingestion.",
 quote:"Pump in your stomach, yeah, yeah, put a tube down, hope you don't get that tube in the person's lungs. ... sometimes big tablets are hard to get up out. It's hard to pump it back out. ... So gastric lavage, you don't wanna do it for anybody that has any sort of esophageal rupture, issues with their stomach, all that dangerous, dangerous.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Prevent Absorption’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-058", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"absorb", skill:"apply",
 concept:"whole-bowel-irrigation", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Whole bowel irrigation for a poisoning is the same procedure as:",
 options:[
  {t:"Colonoscopy preparation: drinking a polyethylene glycol solution until it passes through", correct:true, why:"GoLYTELY or magnesium citrate; the whole gut is flushed."},
  {t:"Gastric lavage with a nasogastric tube, pumping fluid into the stomach and sucking it back out", correct:false, why:"Lavage washes the stomach only."},
  {t:"A single dose of activated charcoal", correct:false, why:"Charcoal adsorbs the poison; irrigation flushes it."},
  {t:"Hemodialysis", correct:false, why:"Dialysis works on the blood, not the gut."},
  {t:"Ipecac-induced vomiting", correct:false, why:"Emesis empties the stomach upward."}],
 teach:"Whole bowel irrigation is what a colonoscopy prep does: the patient drinks a solution such as polyethylene glycol (GoLYTELY) or magnesium citrate and everything passes through. It requires an open gut, with no ileus or blockage, and it is messy, which nursing staff dislike; but it is useful, and experienced emergency teams will probably try it for a massive ingestion.",
 quote:"Has anybody undergone a colonoscopy? That's whole bowel irrigation, OK? You drink something like Golightly, polyethylene glycol or what do we know mag citrate or something ... you've got to make sure people don't have any issues with no ileus, no blockages in the gut.",
 note:"The transcript renders GoLYTELY as \"Golightly\".",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Prevent Absorption’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-059", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"absorb", skill:"apply",
 concept:"activated-charcoal-properties", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statement about activated charcoal is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"It adsorbs only certain compounds, and what it binds leaves in the feces", correct:true, why:"Its chemistry decides what sticks; if the poison does not stick, charcoal is pointless."},
  {t:"It is ordinary charcoal briquettes crushed with a hammer, mixed into water and swallowed", correct:false, why:"It is purified charcoal with very fine particles given as a slurry in water."},
  {t:"It binds every ingested compound", correct:false, why:"Not every compound is grabbed by charcoal."},
  {t:"It is pleasant to take and rarely causes vomiting", correct:false, why:"It is black, nasty and messy, and people often vomit it, risking aspiration."},
  {t:"The bound drug is absorbed into the blood with the charcoal", correct:false, why:"Charcoal is not absorbed; the complex goes out in the feces."}],
 teach:"Activated charcoal is purified charcoal with very fine particles, given as a slurry in water. It is black, unpleasant and messy, and the main risk is that the patient vomits and aspirates charcoal and stomach contents. It adsorbs only certain compounds, so it is useless for poisons that do not stick to it; a compound that does stick leaves the body in the feces.",
 quote:"This is purified charcoal. It's got very fine particles. ... people often vomit, OK? Not a good thing to have vomiting going on because you could aspirate. ... not every compound a person ingests is going to be grabbed and absorbed by charcoal. ... where does it go? Fecal illumination. OK?",
 note:"\"Fecal illumination\" in the transcript is fecal elimination.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Prevent Absorption’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-060", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"absorb", skill:"recall",
 concept:"single-dose-charcoal-timing", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Single-dose activated charcoal probably needs to be given within what time of the ingestion?",
 options:[
  {t:"1 to 2 hours", correct:true, why:"After that the drug has passed the stomach and is being absorbed into the bloodstream."},
  {t:"10 to 15 minutes", correct:false, why:"The window is wider than that: single-dose charcoal works while the poison is still in the stomach, which she put at about 1 to 2 hours."},
  {t:"6 to 8 hours", correct:false, why:"Far too late; the drug has moved on."},
  {t:"24 hours", correct:false, why:"By then the drug has long passed the stomach into the bloodstream; a single dose can bind only what is still in the gut."},
  {t:"Any time, as charcoal pulls drug back out of the blood", correct:false, why:"A single dose works on what is still in the stomach."}],
 teach:"A single dose of activated charcoal has to reach the stomach while the poison is still there, so it is given very soon after ingestion, within about 1 to 2 hours. Wait longer and the drug has gone past the stomach and into the bloodstream.",
 quote:"single dose activated charcoal, we say it probably needs to be done within 1 to 2 hours of somebody ingesting whatever they overdosed on. OK.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Prevent Absorption’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-061", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"absorb", skill:"tell",
 concept:"multiple-dose-charcoal-enterohepatic", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Multiple-dose activated charcoal is reserved for an overdose of a compound that:",
 options:[
  {t:"Sticks to charcoal and undergoes enterohepatic recirculation", correct:true, why:"A later dose catches drug that the liver secretes back into the small intestine through the bile."},
  {t:"Has a very large volume of distribution", correct:false, why:"Vd matters for hemodialysis, not charcoal."},
  {t:"Was swallowed more than 24 hours ago, after single-dose charcoal would be useless", correct:false, why:"Timing does not create the indication; recirculation does."},
  {t:"Is a weak acid trapped in the urine", correct:false, why:"That is ion trapping for renal elimination."},
  {t:"Caused esophageal rupture", correct:false, why:"That is a contraindication to lavage, not an indication for charcoal."}],
 teach:"Some drugs go to the liver, are moved into the bile and re-enter the small intestine, then return to the liver: enterohepatic recirculation (also called gastric recycling). If the overdosed drug recirculates and also sticks to charcoal, a second or later dose of charcoal can capture it as it comes back into the gut, which is why multiple-dose charcoal can be used even after the toxin has been absorbed. Its use is limited to compounds that meet both conditions.",
 quote:"if you give a dose, a second dose of charcoal, you might be able to catch some of that compound as it's being re-injected back into the small intestine from the liver, from the through the bile. ... the use of multiple dose activated charcoal is limited to things that get stuck on charcoal and undergo that recirculation, OK.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Prevent Absorption’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-062", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"absorb", skill:"apply",
 concept:"poison-center-statements", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What have the American and European poison control centers said in the last decade or so about emesis, lavage, whole bowel irrigation and charcoal?",
 options:[
  {t:"They are risky; use them only when really necessary, at the judgment of the emergency team", correct:true, why:"The methods remain available to experienced toxicologists for massive, dangerous ingestions, but none is routine."},
  {t:"All four should be used together for every ingestion", correct:false, why:"The opposite of the statements."},
  {t:"Emesis is preferred over the other three", correct:false, why:"Emesis is the one least likely to be tried; it is no longer used."},
  {t:"Charcoal has replaced the need for any other method", correct:false, why:"Charcoal has its own risks and limits."},
  {t:"They are banned in hospitals, and any emergency team that uses them must report it to the center", correct:false, why:"They are discouraged, not banned; the ER decides."}],
 teach:"Each absorption-prevention method carries its own risk: aspiration with emesis and with vomited charcoal; with lavage, a tube in the lungs, and danger in anyone with esophageal rupture; whole bowel irrigation needs an open gut with no ileus or blockage. Weighing those risks, the American and European poison control centers have said the methods are dangerous and should be used only if really needed, leaving the decision to the ER. For a massive, dangerous ingestion, experienced toxicologists will probably still try lavage and whole bowel irrigation, but probably not emesis.",
 quote:"The American Poison Control Centers and also the European poison control centers have all in the last decade or so put forth statements about whether or not these things, whether or not they think these methods should be used, and they all basically say they're dangerous, they're risky, only use them if you really think you have to. So then it's up to the judgment of the people in the ER.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment More on preventing absorption’ (DiPiro ch. e9); transcript 10/14"},

/* ---------------- Evidence-based guidelines ---------------- */
{id:"L15-063", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"guide", skill:"apply",
 concept:"guideline-data-source", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Where does the evidence come from for the evidence-based consensus guidelines on out-of-hospital management of overdoses?",
 options:[
  {t:"Published case reports and case series, evaluated and graded by expert authors", correct:true, why:"Clinicians write up real overdoses; experts pull every report, evaluate each one and grade the evidence and the recommendations."},
  {t:"Randomized clinical trials in consenting volunteers who agreed to take a measured overdose", correct:false, why:"No one can ethically be consented to overdose."},
  {t:"Large animal overdose studies", correct:false, why:"Animals are used very rarely, only to test whether a treatment works."},
  {t:"Manufacturer package inserts", correct:false, why:"The evidence does not come from manufacturers; it is published case reports and case series of real overdoses, written up by clinicians and evaluated and graded by expert authors."},
  {t:"Poison control center opinion without data", correct:false, why:"The documents are evidence-based and weighted, even though the evidence is case reports."}],
 teach:"No one can ethically be consented to take a massive overdose, so overdose treatment is not tested in clinical trials, and animals are used only rarely, to check whether a treatment works. The evidence comes from real overdoses: clinicians and pharmacists write up cases and publish them, and expert authors pull every case report and case series, evaluate each, and grade both the evidence and the recommendations. That grading is why the documents count as evidence-based without trials.",
 quote:"The data for these evidence-based guidance documents comes from people overdosing, clinicians, pharmacists, doctors write up the case, get it published, and it's in the literature. These people that wrote these guidance documents are experts in the field. They pulled all those documents, evaluated every single one of those case reports or case series, and wrote these evidence-based documents.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Guidelines for Overdoses’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-064", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"guide", skill:"recall", multi:true, lowYield:true,
 concept:"guideline-drug-list", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"For which of the following have evidence-based consensus guidelines for out-of-hospital management been written? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Beta-blockers", correct:true, why:"One of the two guideline documents the class will discuss on 10/19."},
  {t:"Calcium channel blockers", correct:true, why:"The other document for 10/19."},
  {t:"Acetaminophen", correct:true, why:"On the slide's list."},
  {t:"Iron", correct:true, why:"On the slide's list."},
  {t:"Tricyclic antidepressants", correct:true, why:"On the slide's list, as TCAs."},
  {t:"Metronidazole", correct:false, why:"Not on the list; it was her myelinopathy example."},
  {t:"Omeprazole", correct:false, why:"Not on the list; it was her interstitial nephritis example."}],
 teach:"The slide lists the compounds with evidence-based consensus guidelines for out-of-hospital management: acetaminophen, atypical antipsychotics, beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, dextromethorphan, diphenhydramine and dimenhydrinate, iron, methylphenidate, salicylate, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, TCAs, valproic acid, elemental mercury, ethylene glycol and long-acting anticoagulant rodenticides. Only a few drugs have them, but they are nasty ones. The objective is to know which compounds have guidelines and what information the guidelines hold.",
 quote:"Only a few drugs are out there that have these documents written, but these are pretty nasty drugs, OK? And we're going to just look at two of these documents, beta blockers and calcium channel blockers, and talk about them a little bit on Monday, OK?",
 note:"Only beta-blockers and calcium channel blockers were named aloud on 10/14; the rest of the list is from the slide.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Guidelines for Overdoses’; transcript 10/14"},

{id:"L15-065", lecture:"L15", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L15", sub:"guide", skill:"recall",
 concept:"bb-ccb-guidelines-content", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker guideline documents assigned as abstracts differ in two ways that the table assignment asks for. Which two?",
 options:[
  {t:"Beta-blocker versus calcium channel blocker management, and immediate- versus extended-release tablets", correct:true, why:"Slight differences between the two cardiac classes, and different handling of immediate-release and extended-release ingestions."},
  {t:"Adult versus veterinary dosing, and oral versus intravenous antidotes", correct:false, why:"The documents contrast how the two cardiac drug classes are managed and how immediate- and extended-release tablets are handled; dosing populations and antidote routes are not their contrasts."},
  {t:"Single- versus multiple-dose charcoal, and emesis versus lavage", correct:false, why:"Those are absorption-prevention distinctions, not the guideline contrasts she named."},
  {t:"Treatment in the hospital versus at home, and hemodialysis versus hemoperfusion for drugs with a large volume of distribution", correct:false, why:"The documents are about out-of-hospital management; dialysis was not the contrast."},
  {t:"Zone 1 versus zone 3 injury, and acute versus chronic", correct:false, why:"Zones and acute versus chronic describe organ injury, not overdose management; the guideline contrasts are beta-blocker versus calcium channel blocker, and immediate- versus extended-release."}],
 teach:"The two guidance documents say what to do when a patient has swallowed a handful of beta-blocker or a handful of calcium channel blocker, and the answers differ slightly between the two classes even though both are cardiac drugs, and between immediate-release and extended-release tablets. The abstracts summarize what to do; the table is filled in for completion points and discussed on Monday 10/19.",
 quote:"there are slight differences in what we do for a beta blocker overdose compared to a calcium channel overdose, even though they're all cardiac meds, OK? It also. Has differences between immediate release and extended release tablets. What we do, OK, so read the abstract, fill out the table as best you can, and we'll talk about it in class.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; transcript 10/14"}
);
